window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["vector-databases"]=[{"id":"vector-databases-f0001","card":"q-vector-databases-001-vector-scalar","sec":"vector-databases-s003","q":"What is the difference between a scalar and a vector in math, and what does \"vector\" mean in a programming or ML context?","a":"<p>A scalar has magnitude only (a temperature, a price). A vector in the physics sense has magnitude and direction (velocity, force). In programming and ML a vector is a one-dimensional, ordered list of numbers where each position has a meaning. Embeddings are vectors of this kind.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0002","card":"q-vector-databases-001-vector-scalar","sec":"vector-databases-s003","q":"Why must every element of a vector stored in a vector database have the same type?","a":"<p>Distance functions such as L2, cosine and inner product do element-by-element arithmetic across two vectors, so positions must line up and hold the same numeric type. A fixed type also lets the database store vectors in a compact fixed-width layout and use SIMD or GPU math. This is why vector fields are declared as <code>FLOAT_VECTOR</code> or <code>BINARY_VECTOR</code> with a fixed dimension.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0003","card":"q-vector-databases-001-vector-scalar","sec":"vector-databases-s003","q":"In a vector database, what does it mean that two vectors are \"close\"?","a":"<p>It means a distance or similarity metric (L2, cosine, inner product) says they are near each other. For embeddings produced by one model, near vectors represent semantically similar items. Closeness is only meaningful for vectors from the same model and the same metric.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0004","card":"q-vector-databases-002-why-vectorize-text","sec":"vector-databases-s004","q":"Why do you have to convert text into vectors before using it in an ML model or a similarity search?","a":"<p>Models and distance functions work on numbers, not strings. A good embedding turns text into numbers so that meaning is preserved: texts with similar meaning get nearby vectors. That makes it possible to search by meaning instead of by exact words.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0005","card":"q-vector-databases-002-why-vectorize-text","sec":"vector-databases-s004","q":"What is the advantage of semantic search on embeddings over keyword search?","a":"<p>Keyword search needs the same words to appear. Embedding search matches meaning, so \"how do I reset my password\" can find a document titled \"account recovery steps\". The cost is that results are approximate, harder to explain, and need an embedding model plus a vector index. Many production systems combine both (hybrid search).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0006","card":"q-vector-databases-002-why-vectorize-text","sec":"vector-databases-s004","q":"Why must the stored documents and the user's query be embedded with the same embedding model?","a":"<p>Each model defines its own vector space. Dimensions mean different things in different models, and the sizes may differ. Comparing a vector from model A with a vector from model B gives meaningless scores or a dimension-mismatch error. If you change the model you must re-embed the whole corpus.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0007","card":"q-vector-databases-003-vectorization-techniques-oldest-to-newes","sec":"vector-databases-s005","q":"What is the Bag of Words technique, and what are its two main limitations?","a":"<p>Bag of Words builds a vocabulary and represents each text as a count of each vocabulary word. It ignores word order and meaning: \"cat run\" and \"run cat\" get the same vector, and \"car\" and \"automobile\" are as unrelated as \"car\" and \"banana\". The vectors are also sparse and as long as the vocabulary.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0008","card":"q-vector-databases-003-vectorization-techniques-oldest-to-newes","sec":"vector-databases-s005","q":"Given a vocabulary of four words, what vector does Bag of Words produce for the sentence \"cat cat run\"?","a":"<p><code>[2, 0, 1, 0]</code>. Each position is the count of one vocabulary word: cat appears twice, dog zero times, run once, jump zero times.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># Bag of Words: dictionary [&quot;cat&quot;, &quot;dog&quot;, &quot;run&quot;, &quot;jump&quot;]\nsentence1 = &quot;cat run&quot;      # [1, 0, 1, 0]\nsentence3 = &quot;cat cat run&quot;  # [2, 0, 1, 0]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0009","card":"q-vector-databases-003-vectorization-techniques-oldest-to-newes","sec":"vector-databases-s005","q":"How does TF-IDF improve on Bag of Words, and what is still missing?","a":"<p>TF-IDF multiplies term frequency by inverse document frequency. Words that are frequent in one document but rare across the corpus get a high weight, and common words such as \"the\" get a low weight. It still produces sparse vectors and has no understanding of meaning or word order, so synonyms are unrelated.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0010","card":"q-vector-databases-003-vectorization-techniques-oldest-to-newes","sec":"vector-databases-s005","q":"What is the difference between word embeddings (Word2Vec, GloVe, FastText) and sentence embeddings from transformers?","a":"<p>Word embeddings give one dense vector per word, independent of context, so \"bank\" has the same vector in \"river bank\" and \"bank account\". Transformer sentence embeddings produce one vector for a whole sentence or paragraph, computed with full context, so the same word gets different effects in different sentences. Sentence embeddings are the standard for RAG and semantic search.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0011","card":"q-vector-databases-003-vectorization-techniques-oldest-to-newes","sec":"vector-databases-s005","q":"What is the idea behind the \"king - man + woman is about queen\" example in word embeddings?","a":"<p>Word embedding training places words so that relationships become consistent directions in the space. The offset from \"man\" to \"woman\" is similar to the offset from \"king\" to \"queen\". It shows that vectors capture semantic relationships and not only similarity, and that arithmetic on vectors is meaningful.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0012","card":"q-vector-databases-003-vectorization-techniques-oldest-to-newes","sec":"vector-databases-s005","q":"In the <code>sentence-transformers</code> library, what does this code return, and why is that useful?","a":"<p>It loads a pre-trained sentence embedding model and <code>encode</code> returns a dense NumPy vector for the text. <code>all-MiniLM-L6-v2</code> returns 384 dimensions. It runs locally and is free, which makes it a common alternative to a paid embedding API for small and medium projects.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\nmodel = SentenceTransformer(&#x27;all-MiniLM-L6-v2&#x27;)\nembedding = model.encode(&quot;Vector databases store embeddings&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0013","card":"q-vector-databases-003-vectorization-techniques-oldest-to-newes","sec":"vector-databases-s005","q":"When would you pick a locally hosted sentence-transformers model over a hosted embedding API such as OpenAI embeddings?","a":"<p>Pick local when data cannot leave your network, when you want no per-token cost or rate limits, or when you need low latency and predictable availability. Pick the hosted API when you want the highest quality without managing GPUs and the data policy allows it. Remember the vector dimension differs (384 for MiniLM, 1536 for the OpenAI <code>text-embedding-3-small</code>), so the collection schema must match the chosen model.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0014","card":"q-vector-databases-003-vectorization-techniques-oldest-to-newes","sec":"vector-databases-s005","q":"What is the difference between sparse and dense vectors, and which vectorization techniques produce each?","a":"<p>A sparse vector is as long as the vocabulary and almost all entries are zero. Bag of Words and TF-IDF produce sparse vectors. A dense vector is short (hundreds to a few thousand numbers) and nearly every entry is non-zero. Neural embeddings produce dense vectors. A vector database cares because the storage format, index type and distance computation differ: dense vectors use <code>FLOAT_VECTOR</code> with HNSW or IVF indexes, and sparse vectors need a sparse format and sparse index (for example <code>SPARSE_FLOAT_VECTOR</code> in Milvus 2.4 and later).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0015","card":"q-vector-databases-004-dimensions","sec":"vector-databases-s006","q":"What is the trade-off when choosing an embedding model with more dimensions?","a":"<p>More dimensions can encode more information and often give better retrieval quality. The cost is more storage and RAM per vector, slower distance computation and slower index builds. Lower-dimension models (such as 128 to 384) are cheaper and faster but may lose accuracy on subtle meaning; 1536 or more gives the best accuracy at the highest storage and latency. Measure retrieval quality on your own data and pick the smallest size that meets the target.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0016","card":"q-vector-databases-004-dimensions","sec":"vector-databases-s006","q":"What is the curse of dimensionality, and how does it affect vector search?","a":"<p>As dimensions grow, distances between points become more similar to each other, so the nearest and farthest neighbours differ less and exact indexes lose their pruning power. Search gets slower and similarity scores less discriminating. That is why high-dimensional search uses approximate indexes (HNSW, IVF) and why you pick the smallest embedding size that still meets the quality target.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0017","card":"q-vector-databases-004-dimensions","sec":"vector-databases-s006","q":"Roughly how many dimensions do these embedding models produce: Word2Vec, BERT, all-MiniLM-L6-v2, OpenAI <code>text-embedding-ada-002</code>?","a":"<p>Word2Vec 100 to 300, BERT base 768, all-MiniLM-L6-v2 384, OpenAI <code>text-embedding-ada-002</code> 1536. The OpenAI <code>text-embedding-3-small</code> model is also 1536 and <code>text-embedding-3-large</code> is 3072 by default.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0018","card":"q-vector-databases-004-dimensions","sec":"vector-databases-s006","q":"In the OpenAI <code>text-embedding-3</code> models (introduced 2024), how can you reduce the vector size without switching model?","a":"<p>The embeddings API accepts a <code>dimensions</code> parameter that returns a shortened vector. This lowers storage and speeds up search with a small quality loss. Whatever size you choose, the vector database schema must use the same dimension for all stored vectors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0019","card":"q-vector-databases-004-dimensions","sec":"vector-databases-s006","q":"In a vector database, why can you not change the vector dimension of an existing collection?","a":"<p>The index structures and stored segments are built for a fixed dimension, and every inserted vector must match it. To change the dimension (for example after switching embedding models) you create a new collection with the new dimension, re-embed the data, load it and switch the application over.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0020","card":"q-vector-databases-005-distance-similarity-measures","sec":"vector-databases-s007","q":"What do L2, inner product (IP) and cosine measure, and for each one is a higher or lower score better?","a":"<p>L2 is the straight-line (Euclidean) distance, so lower is closer. Inner product is the dot product, so higher is more similar. Cosine similarity is the angle between vectors, range -1 to 1, so higher (closer to 1) is more similar. Cosine ignores vector length.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0021","card":"q-vector-databases-005-distance-similarity-measures","sec":"vector-databases-s007","q":"When two vectors are normalized to length 1, how do cosine similarity, inner product and L2 relate?","a":"<p>Cosine equals the inner product, because the length terms are 1. L2 distance squared equals 2 minus 2 times the inner product, so all three give the same ranking. This is why many systems normalize embeddings and use the faster inner product.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0022","card":"q-vector-databases-005-distance-similarity-measures","sec":"vector-databases-s007","q":"When would you use inner product or L2 instead of cosine similarity for vector search?","a":"<p>Use inner product or L2 when vector length carries meaning, for example a magnitude that encodes confidence, popularity or importance in recommendation embeddings. Cosine ignores length and compares only direction. If the vectors are normalized, all three give the same ranking, so the choice does not matter. Always use the metric the embedding model was trained with.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0023","card":"q-vector-databases-005-distance-similarity-measures","sec":"vector-databases-s007","q":"For text embeddings, why is cosine similarity the usual choice and not L2?","a":"<p>Text meaning is carried by the direction of the vector, and the length can vary with text length or the model. Cosine compares only direction. L2 is also affected by magnitude. Always check which metric the embedding model was trained for.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0024","card":"q-vector-databases-005-distance-similarity-measures","sec":"vector-databases-s007","q":"What does this NumPy function compute, and what does it return for [1, 2, 3] and [4, 5, 6]?","a":"<p>It computes the cosine of the angle between the two vectors: the dot product divided by the product of their lengths. For [1, 2, 3] and [4, 5, 6] the dot product is 32 and the lengths multiply to about 32.83, so the result is about 0.974, meaning nearly the same direction.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import numpy as np\ndef cosine_similarity(v1, v2):  return np.dot(v1, v2) / (np.linalg.norm(v1) * np.linalg.norm(v2))</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0025","card":"q-vector-databases-005-distance-similarity-measures","sec":"vector-databases-s007","q":"In NumPy, what does this function return for [1, 2, 3] and [4, 5, 6], and is a larger value closer or farther?","a":"<p>It returns the straight-line distance, about 5.196 (the square root of 27). It is a distance, so a larger value means farther apart. The subtraction <code>v1 - v2</code> needs NumPy arrays and does not work on plain Python lists.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import numpy as np\ndef euclidean_distance(v1, v2): return np.sqrt(np.sum((v1 - v2) ** 2))</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0026","card":"q-vector-databases-005-distance-similarity-measures","sec":"vector-databases-s007","q":"In Milvus, which metric types can you choose for float vectors and which for binary vectors?","a":"<p>For float vectors: L2, IP and, in Milvus 2.3 and later, COSINE. In earlier 2.x versions you normalized the vectors and used IP to get cosine behaviour. Binary vectors use Hamming or Jaccard distance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0027","card":"q-vector-databases-006-similarity-search-workflow","sec":"vector-databases-s008","q":"What are the steps of a vector similarity search, from a user's query string to the answer?","a":"<ol>\n  <li>Embed the query with the same model that embedded the data. 2) Compare the query vector with the stored vectors using the chosen metric (an ANN index at scale). 3) Rank the results by score. 4) Return the top K, together with their scalar fields or original text.</li>\n</ol>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0028","card":"q-vector-databases-006-similarity-search-workflow","sec":"vector-databases-s008","q":"What does this code do to get the top 3 most similar documents, and why the <code>[::-1]</code>?","a":"<p><code>np.argsort</code> returns indices sorted from the lowest to the highest similarity. <code>[::-1]</code> reverses that to highest first, and <code>[:3]</code> keeps the first three. For a distance metric such as L2 you would not reverse, because lower is better.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>top_k_indices = np.argsort(similarities)[::-1][:3]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0029","card":"q-vector-databases-006-similarity-search-workflow","sec":"vector-databases-s008","q":"Why should you embed the documents once and store them instead of re-embedding the documents on every query?","a":"<p>Embedding is the slow and, with an API, paid part. Pre-computing and storing vectors means a query needs only one embedding call (for the query text) plus an index lookup. Re-embedding all documents per query makes latency and cost grow with the corpus size.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0030","card":"q-vector-databases-007-approximate-nearest-neighbor-ann","sec":"vector-databases-s009","q":"What is vector quantization (scalar and product quantization), and what is the trade-off?","a":"<p>Quantization compresses stored vectors. Scalar quantization (SQ) stores each dimension in fewer bits, for example 8-bit integers instead of 32-bit floats, which cuts memory about 4 times. Product quantization (PQ) splits a vector into <code>m</code> sub-vectors and replaces each with the id of its nearest centroid in a small codebook, which compresses much more. The cost is lower accuracy, because distances are computed on approximations. Use it when the data does not fit in RAM, often combined with IVF (IVF_SQ8, IVF_PQ), and measure the recall loss.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0031","card":"q-vector-databases-007-approximate-nearest-neighbor-ann","sec":"vector-databases-s009","q":"Why is exact nearest-neighbour search a problem at scale, and what does ANN do about it?","a":"<p>Exact (brute-force) search compares the query with every vector, which is O(n) per query, too slow for millions of vectors. ANN uses an index (graph, clusters, hashing) so that it examines only a small part of the data. It trades a small loss of accuracy (recall) for a large gain in speed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0032","card":"q-vector-databases-007-approximate-nearest-neighbor-ann","sec":"vector-databases-s009","q":"What does \"recall\" mean for an ANN index and why can a recall under 100% be acceptable?","a":"<p>Recall is the fraction of the true nearest neighbours that the approximate search actually returns. For retrieval in RAG or recommendations, missing a few of the top 10 neighbours rarely changes the answer, while a large latency saving does. You tune index parameters to reach the recall your use case needs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0033","card":"q-vector-databases-007-approximate-nearest-neighbor-ann","sec":"vector-databases-s009","q":"Name the main ANN algorithm families and the idea behind each.","a":"<p>HNSW builds a multi-layer graph and greedily walks it toward the query. IVF clusters the vectors and searches only the closest clusters. LSH hashes similar vectors into the same buckets. ANNOY builds a forest of random-projection trees. Also PQ and SQ compress vectors to save memory, and are often combined with IVF.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0034","card":"q-vector-databases-007-approximate-nearest-neighbor-ann","sec":"vector-databases-s009","q":"When is it better to use exact search (a FLAT index) instead of ANN?","a":"<p>When the dataset is small (roughly tens of thousands of vectors), because exact search is already fast and gives 100% recall. It is also right when accuracy is critical and the latency budget allows it, or as a ground-truth baseline to measure the recall of an ANN index.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0035","card":"q-vector-databases-008-pitfalls","sec":"vector-databases-s010","q":"What are the most common mistakes when building a similarity search system with embeddings?","a":"<p>Mixing embedding models between data and query, a dimension mismatch, using the wrong metric for the data, not normalizing vectors when the metric assumes it, re-embedding at query time instead of storing vectors, and inconsistent preprocessing (different cleaning or chunking between indexing and querying).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0036","card":"q-vector-databases-008-pitfalls","sec":"vector-databases-s010","q":"Why should the embedding model version be tracked together with the stored vectors?","a":"<p>A new model version produces vectors in a different space, so old and new vectors cannot be compared. If you upgrade silently, search quality drops with no error. Record the model name and version (for example as metadata or in the collection name) so you know when a full re-embedding is needed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0037","card":"q-vector-databases-008-pitfalls","sec":"vector-databases-s010","q":"You want to upgrade to a better embedding model for a live search system. How do you do it safely?","a":"<p>Create a new collection (the dimension may change), embed the whole corpus with the new model, build the index and load it, then compare quality on a test set. Switch queries to the new collection, which also uses the new model for query embedding, and keep the old collection for rollback until you are satisfied. Never mix old and new vectors in one collection.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0038","card":"q-vector-databases-009-why-not-a-traditional-db","sec":"vector-databases-s011","q":"Why is a traditional relational database a poor fit for storing and searching embeddings?","a":"<p>It has no ANN indexes, so a similarity query is a full scan with a distance computation per row. B-tree indexes do not help with high-dimensional distances, there are no built-in vector metrics, and it does not scale to hundreds of millions or billions of vectors. A vector database adds ANN indexes, metrics and distributed storage for this workload.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0039","card":"q-vector-databases-009-why-not-a-traditional-db","sec":"vector-databases-s011","q":"What does a vector database give you that a plain NumPy array of embeddings in memory does not?","a":"<p>Persistence, scalable ANN indexes instead of brute force, metadata filtering, CRUD operations, concurrency, replication and access control. A NumPy array is fine for a prototype with a few thousand vectors, but it has no durability and does not scale past one machine's memory.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0040","card":"q-vector-databases-009-why-not-a-traditional-db","sec":"vector-databases-s011","q":"When can an existing database with a vector extension, such as PostgreSQL with <code>pgvector</code>, be enough instead of a dedicated vector database?","a":"<p>When you already run that database, the data set is up to a few million vectors, and you want one system with transactions and joins between metadata and vectors. A dedicated vector database is better for very large collections, high query rates, heavy ANN tuning, or independent scaling of the vector workload.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0041","card":"q-vector-databases-010-key-features","sec":"vector-databases-s012","q":"Which data types does a vector database such as Milvus store?","a":"<p>Float vectors (dense embeddings), binary vectors (compact bit-packed), and ordinary scalar fields such as integers, floats, strings (VARCHAR), booleans, JSON and arrays. Storing scalars next to vectors lets you filter by metadata during a similarity search.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0042","card":"q-vector-databases-010-key-features","sec":"vector-databases-s012","q":"How do the consistency and transaction guarantees of a vector database compare with an RDBMS?","a":"<p>A vector database offers CRUD and some isolation, but not the full multi-row ACID transactions of an RDBMS. It is tuned for read-heavy workloads, and Milvus lets you choose a consistency level per request. Do not use it as the system of record for money or inventory.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0043","card":"q-vector-databases-010-key-features","sec":"vector-databases-s012","q":"In pymilvus, what do <code>anns_field</code>, <code>param</code> and <code>limit</code> do in this search call?","a":"<p><code>data</code> is a list of query vectors. <code>anns_field</code> names the vector field to search. <code>param</code> holds the metric and the index-specific search parameters (here <code>nprobe</code>, the number of clusters to probe for an IVF index). <code>limit</code> is the top-K, so 5 results per query vector. The metric must match the one used when the index was built.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>collection.search(\n    data=[query_vector],\n    anns_field=&quot;embedding&quot;,\n    param={&quot;metric_type&quot;: &quot;COSINE&quot;, &quot;params&quot;: {&quot;nprobe&quot;: 10}},\n    limit=5\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0044","card":"q-vector-databases-010-key-features","sec":"vector-databases-s012","q":"When would you store binary vectors instead of float vectors, and which metrics do you use with them?","a":"<p>Binary vectors hold bits, so each dimension costs 1 bit instead of 4 bytes, about 32 times less memory. Use them when the data is naturally binary, such as perceptual image hashes, fingerprints or binary-quantized embeddings, and when you need very fast, cheap search at some loss of precision. The metrics are Hamming distance (number of differing bits) and Jaccard distance (overlap of set bits), not L2 or cosine.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0045","card":"q-vector-databases-010-key-features","sec":"vector-databases-s012","q":"Why is a built-in similarity search the distinguishing feature of a vector database?","a":"<p>Without it you would write custom code to compute distances, rank results and build ANN indexes. A vector database gives you metrics and indexes as configuration, executed close to the data and in parallel across nodes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0046","card":"q-vector-databases-011-categories-and-examples","sec":"vector-databases-s013","q":"How can vector databases be categorized, and what are examples in each category?","a":"<p>By licensing: open source (Milvus, Qdrant, Weaviate, Chroma) versus commercial (Pinecone, Zilliz Cloud). By architecture: purpose-built vector databases versus general databases with a vector add-on (PostgreSQL with pgvector, Redis search, Elasticsearch dense vectors). By deployment: embedded, local server or managed cloud.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0047","card":"q-vector-databases-011-categories-and-examples","sec":"vector-databases-s013","q":"What is an embedded vector database such as Chroma good for, and when does it stop being enough?","a":"<p>It runs inside your application process with no server, so it is ideal for prototypes, notebooks, tests and small local apps. It stops being enough when you need multiple clients, high availability, horizontal scaling or strong operational features. Then use a server or managed service such as Milvus or Pinecone.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0048","card":"q-vector-databases-011-categories-and-examples","sec":"vector-databases-s013","q":"What is the difference between Milvus and Zilliz Cloud?","a":"<p>Milvus is the open-source vector database you run yourself, standalone or as a cluster. Zilliz Cloud is the commercial managed service built on Milvus, so you do not operate the servers. The client APIs are compatible, which makes moving between them easy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0049","card":"q-vector-databases-011-categories-and-examples","sec":"vector-databases-s013","q":"What are the trade-offs between a managed vector database (Pinecone, Zilliz Cloud) and self-hosting (Milvus, Qdrant)?","a":"<p>Managed means less operational work, easy scaling and SLAs, but ongoing cost, less control, and data in a vendor's cloud. Self-hosting gives control, data locality and lower cost at scale, but you handle upgrades, monitoring, backups and scaling.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0050","card":"q-vector-databases-012-advantages","sec":"vector-databases-s014","q":"What are the main benefits of using a vector database for an LLM application?","a":"<p>Semantic search with ANN indexes, low-latency retrieval from large collections, bulk loading, horizontal scaling and fault tolerance, and combining vector search with scalar filters. It lets the application add its own data to LLM answers (RAG) without fine-tuning.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0051","card":"q-vector-databases-012-advantages","sec":"vector-databases-s014","q":"In pymilvus, what does <code>create_index</code> do, and why does the choice of index parameters matter?","a":"<p>It builds an ANN index on the named vector field so searches do not scan every vector. <code>index_type</code> selects the algorithm (HNSW here), <code>metric_type</code> fixes how distance is computed, and <code>params</code> tune build quality against memory and build time. Wrong choices give either low recall or high memory and latency.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>collection.create_index(\n    field_name=&quot;embedding&quot;,\n    index_params={&quot;metric_type&quot;: &quot;COSINE&quot;, &quot;index_type&quot;: &quot;HNSW&quot;,\n                  &quot;params&quot;: {&quot;M&quot;: 16, &quot;efConstruction&quot;: 200}}\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0052","card":"q-vector-databases-012-advantages","sec":"vector-databases-s014","q":"How does a vector database scale to billions of vectors?","a":"<p>By splitting data into segments and shards that are spread across worker nodes, building indexes per segment, and searching segments in parallel, then merging the partial top-K lists. Storage, query and index work can scale separately in a cluster, with replicas for fault tolerance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0053","card":"q-vector-databases-013-shortcomings","sec":"vector-databases-s015","q":"What are the main limitations of a vector database compared with an RDBMS?","a":"<p>Limited or no joins, only basic aggregations, no subqueries or window functions, few built-in string and date functions, weaker transactions (no strong multi-row ACID), slower ingestion because indexes must be built, higher memory use because indexes live in RAM, and fewer integrations with BI and ORM tools.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0054","card":"q-vector-databases-013-shortcomings","sec":"vector-databases-s015","q":"How do you estimate the memory a float vector collection needs?","a":"<p>Raw size is vectors times dimensions times 4 bytes (float32). For 1 million vectors of 768 dimensions that is about 3 GB. Add index overhead, often 30 to 50% for graph or cluster indexes, so plan for about 4.5 GB. Quantized indexes such as IVF_SQ8 or IVF_PQ cut this a lot at some accuracy cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0055","card":"q-vector-databases-013-shortcomings","sec":"vector-databases-s015","q":"Why is ingestion into a vector database slower than into an RDBMS, and how do you reduce the impact?","a":"<p>Each insert has to be added to an ANN index (or the index rebuilt for new segments), and the data must be embedded first. Use bulk or batched inserts, create the index after the bulk load instead of before, and batch embedding calls.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0056","card":"q-vector-databases-013-shortcomings","sec":"vector-databases-s015","q":"Which workloads should not use a vector database as the primary store?","a":"<p>Financial transactions, inventory and other critical state that needs strong ACID guarantees, heavy aggregations and reporting, and purely relational data such as users, orders and catalogs. Keep these in an RDBMS or warehouse and use the vector DB only for similarity search.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0057","card":"q-vector-databases-013-shortcomings","sec":"vector-databases-s015","q":"When might you need a GPU for a vector database workload?","a":"<p>Similarity math is compute-heavy. For very high query throughput or large index builds, GPU index types such as <code>GPU_IVF_FLAT</code> speed up both. They need a GPU-enabled deployment, so use them only when CPU indexes cannot meet the latency or throughput target.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0058","card":"q-vector-databases-014-when-to-use-not-use","sec":"vector-databases-s016","q":"Which use cases fit a vector database, and which do not?","a":"<p>Good fits: semantic search, RAG, recommendations, image and video similarity, anomaly detection and deduplication. Poor fits: transactional workloads, heavy aggregation and reporting, and purely relational data lookups.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0059","card":"q-vector-databases-014-when-to-use-not-use","sec":"vector-databases-s016","q":"How does the hybrid design of keeping metadata in an RDBMS and embeddings in a vector database work, and what is the main risk?","a":"<p>The vector database finds the closest ids, and the RDBMS returns the full records for those ids. The risk is consistency: the two writes are not one transaction, so a failure can leave a row in one store only. Mitigate with a shared id, retries or an outbox pattern, and reconciliation jobs.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class HybridStore:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.postgres = PostgresDB()\n        self.milvus = MilvusDB()\n    def insert(self, doc):\n        self.postgres.insert(doc.metadata)\n        self.milvus.insert(doc.id, doc.embedding)\n    def search(self, query):\n        ids = self.milvus.search(query)\n        return self.postgres.get_by_ids(ids)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0060","card":"q-vector-databases-014-when-to-use-not-use","sec":"vector-databases-s016","q":"A team wants to store all customer order data in a vector database because they plan to add semantic search. What is your advice?","a":"<p>Keep orders in the transactional database, which needs ACID and joins. Store only the embeddings of the searchable text in a vector database along with an id that points back to the order. Search finds ids and the main database returns the order details.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0061","card":"q-vector-databases-015-selection-guide-metric-by-use-case","sec":"vector-databases-s017","q":"How do you choose the distance metric for a vector search use case?","a":"<p>Use the metric the embedding model was trained with (cosine for most text embedding models). For image embeddings and spatial data L2 is common. If the choice is unclear, evaluate each metric on a labelled query set using recall@k and keep the best. Normalized vectors make inner product rank like cosine at lower cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0062","card":"q-vector-databases-016-4-milvus-deployment-architecture-and-set","sec":"vector-databases-s018","q":"What is Milvus and what kinds of data does it store?","a":"<p>Milvus is an open-source vector database for storing, indexing and searching embeddings. It stores vector fields and scalar fields (ints, strings, JSON and so on) in the same collection, so you can combine similarity search with metadata filters. It supports L2, IP and COSINE metrics and has SDKs for Python, Node.js, Go and Java.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0063","card":"q-vector-databases-016-4-milvus-deployment-architecture-and-set","sec":"vector-databases-s018","q":"What are the Milvus deployment options, and when do you choose each?","a":"<p>Standalone runs all components in one process (or one container): simple, good for development and small workloads. Cluster mode runs the components as separate services on Kubernetes for scale and fault tolerance. Zilliz Cloud is the managed commercial service for teams that do not want to operate it. Lite (<code>milvus-lite</code>) is an embedded option for notebooks and tests in recent pymilvus versions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0064","card":"q-vector-databases-017-architecture","sec":"vector-databases-s019","q":"What are the main components of the Milvus architecture and what does each do?","a":"<p>The access layer is the gRPC entry point (port 19530). Coordinators manage the cluster and schedule work. Worker nodes do the data processing, index building and search. etcd stores metadata such as schemas, users and indexes. A message queue (RocksMQ by default in standalone, or Kafka or Pulsar) carries the write log. An object store (MinIO or S3) holds the vector data, index files and logs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0065","card":"q-vector-databases-017-architecture","sec":"vector-databases-s019","q":"In Milvus, why are storage and compute separated, and what is the benefit?","a":"<p>Worker nodes are stateless over the object storage, with metadata in etcd and the write log in a message queue. So you can scale query, data and index workers independently, replace a failed node without losing data, and use cheap object storage for persistence.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0066","card":"q-vector-databases-017-architecture","sec":"vector-databases-s019","q":"In Milvus, what path does a DDL request (create collection, create index, create user) take?","a":"<p>The client sends it to the access layer, which forwards it to the coordinator. The coordinator records the change in etcd, where metadata lives.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0067","card":"q-vector-databases-017-architecture","sec":"vector-databases-s019","q":"In Milvus, what path does a write (insert, update, delete) take?","a":"<p>The client sends it to the access layer, which puts it on the message queue as a log entry. Data worker nodes consume it, and the data is persisted to the object store (MinIO). The queue decouples the writers from the persistence, which gives durability and ordering.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0068","card":"q-vector-databases-017-architecture","sec":"vector-databases-s019","q":"In Milvus, what path does a search request take?","a":"<p>The client sends it to the access layer. The coordinator checks metadata, such as that the collection exists and is loaded. Query worker nodes then search the loaded index segments, merge partial results and return the top K.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0069","card":"q-vector-databases-017-architecture","sec":"vector-databases-s019","q":"Which component stores the schema and index definitions in Milvus, and what happens if it is lost?","a":"<p>etcd stores that metadata. Without it Milvus does not know which collections, schemas and segments exist, even though the data files are in object storage. So etcd needs backup and, in production, a replicated deployment.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0070","card":"q-vector-databases-018-docker-compose-exercise-file-milvus-stan","sec":"vector-databases-s020","q":"Which containers make up a Milvus standalone deployment in Docker Compose, and what is each for?","a":"<p>etcd (metadata), MinIO (object storage for data and index files), the Milvus standalone server (the API, coordination and workers in one process) and, optionally, Attu (a web UI for browsing and managing collections). Standalone needs etcd and MinIO started first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0071","card":"q-vector-databases-018-docker-compose-exercise-file-milvus-stan","sec":"vector-databases-s020","q":"In a Docker Compose file, what does a <code>healthcheck</code> do, and why is it useful for the etcd service?","a":"<p>Compose runs the <code>test</code> command every <code>interval</code>. <code>timeout</code> is the time limit for one check to finish. After <code>retries</code> consecutive failed checks, the container is marked unhealthy. This lets Docker and dependent services know the service is actually ready, not only started, and lets orchestration restart it.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>healthcheck:\n  test: [&quot;CMD&quot;, &quot;etcdctl&quot;, &quot;endpoint&quot;, &quot;health&quot;]\n  interval: 30s\n  timeout: 20s\n  retries: 3</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0072","card":"q-vector-databases-018-docker-compose-exercise-file-milvus-stan","sec":"vector-databases-s020","q":"In Docker Compose, why does a service mount a host directory like this one into the container?","a":"<p>Container file systems are lost when the container is removed. A bind mount keeps the etcd data on the host, so metadata survives <code>down</code> and <code>up</code>. <code>${DOCKER_VOLUME_DIRECTORY:-.}</code> is variable substitution with a default: it uses the environment variable if set, else the current directory.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>volumes:\n  - ${DOCKER_VOLUME_DIRECTORY:-.}/volumes/etcd:/etcd</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0073","card":"q-vector-databases-018-docker-compose-exercise-file-milvus-stan","sec":"vector-databases-s020","q":"Why should the image tags in a Milvus Docker Compose file be pinned to exact versions?","a":"<p>Milvus and its dependencies change quickly, and the client library must be compatible with the server version. Pinning makes the environment reproducible. A floating <code>latest</code> tag may upgrade the server and break the client code or the stored data format.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0074","card":"q-vector-databases-018-docker-compose-exercise-file-milvus-stan","sec":"vector-databases-s020","q":"In a Compose file, why does the MinIO service use default credentials like <code>minioadmin</code>, and what must you change in production?","a":"<p>The defaults are for local development convenience only. In production set strong credentials through secrets or environment variables, or use a managed object store such as S3 with IAM roles, and do not expose the MinIO ports publicly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0075","card":"q-vector-databases-019-docker-compose-exercise-file-milvus-stan","sec":"vector-databases-s020","q":"In Docker Compose, what does <code>depends_on</code> guarantee for the Milvus standalone service?","a":"<p>It controls start order: etcd and MinIO containers are started before Milvus. In its short form it does not wait until they are healthy, only until they are started. To wait for health use the long form with <code>condition: service_healthy</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>depends_on:\n  - &quot;etcd&quot;\n  - &quot;minio&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0076","card":"q-vector-databases-019-docker-compose-exercise-file-milvus-stan","sec":"vector-databases-s020","q":"What does the port mapping <code>\"8000:3000\"</code> mean for the Attu service in Docker Compose?","a":"<p>The format is <code>host:container</code>. Port 3000 inside the container is published on port 8000 of the host, so you open Attu at <code>http://localhost:8000</code>. Changing the left number changes the host port without touching the container.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ports:\n  - &quot;8000:3000&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0077","card":"q-vector-databases-019-docker-compose-exercise-file-milvus-stan","sec":"vector-databases-s020","q":"In the Milvus standalone compose file, which environment variables connect the server to its dependencies?","a":"<p>They tell Milvus where to find etcd and the object store. The hostnames <code>etcd</code> and <code>minio</code> are the Compose service names, which Docker's internal DNS resolves on the shared network, so no IP addresses are needed.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>environment:\n  ETCD_ENDPOINTS: etcd:2379\n  MINIO_ADDRESS: minio:9000</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0078","card":"q-vector-databases-019-docker-compose-exercise-file-milvus-stan","sec":"vector-databases-s020","q":"How do you start, inspect, troubleshoot and stop a Docker Compose stack such as Milvus?","a":"<p><code>docker-compose -f &lt;file&gt; up -d</code> starts it in the background. <code>docker ps</code> lists running containers. <code>docker-compose -f &lt;file&gt; logs</code> shows logs and <code>restart</code> restarts services. <code>down</code> stops and removes the containers (bind-mounted data stays on disk). Newer Docker installs use <code>docker compose</code> (with a space) as a plugin.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0079","card":"q-vector-databases-019-docker-compose-exercise-file-milvus-stan","sec":"vector-databases-s020","q":"Which ports does a Milvus standalone deployment expose and for what?","a":"<p>19530 is the gRPC API used by SDKs. 9091 serves health checks and metrics (for example <code>/healthz</code>). The MinIO console is on 9001 and the S3 API on 9000. Attu is a separate UI container.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0080","card":"q-vector-databases-019-docker-compose-exercise-file-milvus-stan","sec":"vector-databases-s020","q":"What is Attu, and when is it useful?","a":"<p>Attu is the web administration UI for Milvus. It shows databases, collections, schemas and indexes, runs queries, previews data, manages users and roles, and loads or drops collections. It is useful for inspecting and debugging data without writing code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0081","card":"q-vector-databases-020-python-environment-anaconda-python-3-11","sec":"vector-databases-s021","q":"Why do you pin <code>pymilvus</code> to a version that matches the Milvus server, such as 2.3.x with Milvus 2.3?","a":"<p>The client and server protocol and APIs change between releases. A mismatched client can fail with errors or lack features, and in 2.4 and later the recommended API moved to <code>MilvusClient</code>. Pin both and upgrade together after testing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0082","card":"q-vector-databases-021-5-milvus-objects","sec":"vector-databases-s022","q":"What is the object hierarchy in Milvus, and how does it map to an RDBMS?","a":"<p>Connection, then database, then collection, then partition, then entity and fields. A collection is like a table, an entity is like a row and a field is like a column. A database groups collections the way a database does in an RDBMS, and partitions are a Milvus-specific subdivision for faster filtered searches.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0083","card":"q-vector-databases-021-5-milvus-objects","sec":"vector-databases-s022","q":"In Milvus, what is an entity?","a":"<p>A single record in a collection: one value for every field in the schema, including the primary key, the vector and any scalar fields. It corresponds to a row in a relational table.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0084","card":"q-vector-databases-022-connection","sec":"vector-databases-s023","q":"In pymilvus, what is the role of <code>connections.connect</code> and the alias, and why use <code>using=</code> afterwards?","a":"<p><code>connect</code> opens a gRPC connection to the Milvus server and registers it under an alias (<code>\"learn\"</code>). Later calls such as <code>Collection(..., using=\"learn\")</code> take the alias to know which server to use. This makes it possible to work with several servers in one process. If you omit the alias, the name <code>default</code> is used.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from pymilvus import connections\nconnections.connect(&quot;learn&quot;, host=&quot;localhost&quot;, port=&quot;19530&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0085","card":"q-vector-databases-022-connection","sec":"vector-databases-s023","q":"How do you configure Milvus connections for several environments (dev, staging) in one Python process?","a":"<p>Register each one with its own alias, for example with <code>connections.connect(alias, host=..., port=...)</code>, and pass <code>using=&lt;alias&gt;</code> to every call. Keep the alias in a variable or configuration, not hard-coded across the code, so switching environments is a config change.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0086","card":"q-vector-databases-022-connection","sec":"vector-databases-s023","q":"Which connection settings does pymilvus need to reach a Milvus server, and how do you connect to a secured one?","a":"<p>Host and port are required (19530 by default). For a secured server also supply a user and password (or a token). For a managed service like Zilliz Cloud you use its URI and an API token, over TLS. Take the credentials from environment variables or a secret store, not from source code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0087","card":"q-vector-databases-022-connection","sec":"vector-databases-s023","q":"A pymilvus call fails with a connection error. What do you check first?","a":"<p>That the Milvus container is healthy (<code>docker ps</code>, the <code>/healthz</code> endpoint on 9091), that the host and port are right, that the alias used in <code>using=</code> was connected, and that the client version matches the server. Then check firewall or Docker network rules.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0088","card":"q-vector-databases-023-databases","sec":"vector-databases-s024","q":"In Milvus, what is a database and why would you create more than one?","a":"<p>A database is a logical grouping of collections, with its own users and permissions. Multiple databases give isolation: one per tenant, team or environment, with separate access control. The default database is called <code>default</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0089","card":"q-vector-databases-023-databases","sec":"vector-databases-s024","q":"In pymilvus, why must you call <code>using_database</code> after creating a database?","a":"<p>Creating a database does not switch to it. <code>using_database</code> sets the active database for that connection, so later collection operations act on <code>tenant_a</code> instead of <code>default</code>. Forgetting it means collections get created in the wrong database.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from pymilvus import db\ndb.create_database(&quot;tenant_a&quot;, using=&quot;learn&quot;)\ndb.using_database(&quot;tenant_a&quot;, using=&quot;learn&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0090","card":"q-vector-databases-023-databases","sec":"vector-databases-s024","q":"What happens if you try to drop a Milvus database that still contains collections?","a":"<p>The drop is refused. You must drop all its collections first, then call <code>drop_database</code>. This protects against accidentally deleting data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0091","card":"q-vector-databases-023-databases","sec":"vector-databases-s024","q":"In Milvus multitenancy, how do you choose between a database per tenant, a collection per tenant, and a partition per tenant?","a":"<p>Database per tenant gives the strongest isolation and separate access control but has a low count limit (the default maximum is 64 databases). Collection per tenant is flexible but costs memory and metadata for each one. Partition or partition-key per tenant scales to many tenants and is the most economical, but isolation is logical only. Choose by number of tenants and isolation requirements.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0092","card":"q-vector-databases-024-users-and-roles-access-control","sec":"vector-databases-s025","q":"How does role-based access control work in Milvus?","a":"<p>You create users, and roles carry privileges on resources (collections, databases, cluster operations). You assign roles to users, so permissions are managed in one place. There is a built-in <code>public</code> role that every user gets and an <code>admin</code> role with full rights, and you can define custom roles.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0093","card":"q-vector-databases-024-users-and-roles-access-control","sec":"vector-databases-s025","q":"In pymilvus, what do these lines do?","a":"<p><code>create_user</code> creates a user with a password. <code>Role(\"public\")</code> is a handle to the existing <code>public</code> role, and <code>add_user</code> assigns the user to it. Note that the role must grant privileges, otherwise the user can connect but do nothing useful.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from pymilvus import Role, utility\nutility.create_user(&quot;app_user&quot;, &quot;StrongPassword&quot;, using=&quot;learn&quot;)\npublic_role = Role(&quot;public&quot;, using=&quot;learn&quot;)\npublic_role.add_user(&quot;app_user&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0094","card":"q-vector-databases-024-users-and-roles-access-control","sec":"vector-databases-s025","q":"In Milvus, how do you give a role permission to search one collection, and why prefer this over the admin role for an application?","a":"<p>In pymilvus 2.3 you create a custom role, then <code>grant(object_type, object_name, privilege)</code> gives it one privilege on one resource. <code>revoke</code> removes it. Then add users to the role. An application that only searches should have only that privilege (least privilege), so a bug or leaked credential cannot drop collections or delete data.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from pymilvus import Role\nrole = Role(&quot;reader&quot;, using=&quot;learn&quot;)\nrole.create()\nrole.grant(&quot;Collection&quot;, &quot;courses_list&quot;, &quot;Search&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0095","card":"q-vector-databases-024-users-and-roles-access-control","sec":"vector-databases-s025","q":"Why is it not enough to create users in Milvus to secure it?","a":"<p>Authentication must also be enabled on the server (for Milvus 2.3 set <code>common.security.authorizationEnabled</code> to true in the configuration). Until then any client can connect as root. Also change the default root password, use least-privilege roles for applications, and enable TLS.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0096","card":"q-vector-databases-025-collection-schema","sec":"vector-databases-s026","q":"What defines a Milvus collection schema, and what rules apply?","a":"<p>A schema is a list of fields: scalar fields, at least one vector field, and exactly one primary key field. Each field has a name and data type. VARCHAR fields need <code>max_length</code> and vector fields need <code>dim</code>. Optionally you enable dynamic fields for extra attributes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0097","card":"q-vector-databases-025-collection-schema","sec":"vector-databases-s026","q":"In pymilvus, what do <code>FieldSchema</code>, <code>CollectionSchema</code> and <code>Collection</code> each do?","a":"<p><code>FieldSchema</code> describes one field (name, type, size, primary key flag). <code>CollectionSchema</code> bundles the fields plus options like <code>description</code> and dynamic fields. <code>Collection</code> creates the collection on the server from the schema, or opens it if it already exists.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>schema = CollectionSchema(fields=[course_id, title, description, desc_embedding],\n                          description=&quot;Courses List&quot;, enable_dynamic_field=True)\ncollection = Collection(name=&quot;courses_list&quot;, schema=schema, using=&quot;learn&quot;, shard_num=2)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0098","card":"q-vector-databases-025-collection-schema","sec":"vector-databases-s026","q":"In Milvus, what does <code>dim</code> on a <code>FLOAT_VECTOR</code> field have to match, and how do you find it?","a":"<p>It must equal the output size of the embedding model. Find it by embedding a test string and taking the length, for example <code>len(embeddings_model.embed_query(\"test\"))</code> with a LangChain embeddings object. A wrong value makes inserts fail with a dimension error.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>FieldSchema(name=&quot;desc_embedding&quot;, dtype=DataType.FLOAT_VECTOR, dim=1536)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0099","card":"q-vector-databases-025-collection-schema","sec":"vector-databases-s026","q":"In Milvus, what does <code>shard_num</code> control and how do you choose it?","a":"<p>It sets the number of shards, which spreads write (DML) load across data nodes for parallelism. More shards help write throughput but add overhead, so do not over-provision. It is fixed at creation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0100","card":"q-vector-databases-025-collection-schema","sec":"vector-databases-s026","q":"What is the difference between a primary key with <code>auto_id=True</code> and one with <code>auto_id=False</code> in Milvus?","a":"<p>With <code>auto_id=False</code> you supply the ids yourself, which is needed when the id links to another system or you want to upsert by id. With <code>auto_id=True</code> Milvus assigns unique ids and you must leave that column out of the insert data. Use auto ids for data with no natural key, such as cache entries.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>FieldSchema(name=&quot;course_id&quot;, dtype=DataType.INT64, is_primary=True, auto_id=False)\nFieldSchema(name=&quot;cache_id&quot;,  dtype=DataType.INT64, is_primary=True, auto_id=True)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0101","card":"q-vector-databases-025-collection-schema","sec":"vector-databases-s026","q":"Which data types can be a primary key in Milvus?","a":"<p><code>INT64</code> and <code>VARCHAR</code>. A VARCHAR primary key needs <code>max_length</code>. A collection has exactly one primary key and it is unique per entity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0102","card":"q-vector-databases-025-collection-schema","sec":"vector-databases-s026","q":"What are dynamic fields in Milvus and what is the trade-off?","a":"<p>With <code>enable_dynamic_field=True</code>, inserts may include extra keys not in the schema, and Milvus stores them in a hidden JSON field. It gives flexibility for evolving metadata. Dynamic fields are slower to filter on and less controlled than declared fields, so enable them only when needed and declare the fields you filter on often.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0103","card":"q-vector-databases-025-collection-schema","sec":"vector-databases-s026","q":"Which scalar and vector data types does Milvus support, and what extra parameters do <code>VARCHAR</code>, <code>ARRAY</code> and <code>BINARY_VECTOR</code> need?","a":"<p>Scalars: integers (INT8 to INT64), FLOAT, DOUBLE, BOOL, VARCHAR, JSON and ARRAY. Vectors: FLOAT_VECTOR and BINARY_VECTOR. <code>VARCHAR</code> needs <code>max_length</code>. <code>ARRAY</code> needs <code>element_type</code> and <code>max_capacity</code>. <code>BINARY_VECTOR</code> needs a <code>dim</code> that is a multiple of 8, because bits are packed into bytes.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>FieldSchema(name=&quot;tags&quot;, dtype=DataType.ARRAY, element_type=DataType.VARCHAR, max_capacity=10)\nFieldSchema(name=&quot;binary_embedding&quot;, dtype=DataType.BINARY_VECTOR, dim=128)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0104","card":"q-vector-databases-025-collection-schema","sec":"vector-databases-s026","q":"A Milvus collection is already created and in use. Which schema changes are possible and how do you do the others?","a":"<p>In Milvus 2.3 you cannot change field names or types, the primary key or the vector dimension, or remove fields. You can add dynamic fields at insert and edit the description. For other changes create a new collection with the new schema, migrate the data, switch the application and drop the old one. Newer Milvus releases add limited support for adding fields, so check the version's docs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0105","card":"q-vector-databases-025-collection-schema","sec":"vector-databases-s026","q":"How do you check whether a collection exists, list collections and read the number of entities in pymilvus?","a":"<p><code>utility.has_collection</code> returns a boolean, <code>utility.list_collections</code> returns the names, and on a <code>Collection</code> object <code>num_entities</code> gives the entity count. A guard such as <code>has_collection</code> before creating makes a notebook or deployment script safe to run again.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from pymilvus import utility\nutility.has_collection(&quot;courses_list&quot;, using=&quot;learn&quot;)\nutility.list_collections(using=&quot;learn&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0106","card":"q-vector-databases-026-partitions","sec":"vector-databases-s027","q":"What is a partition in Milvus, and what problem does it solve?","a":"<p>A partition is a physical subdivision of a collection. A search limited to some partitions reads less data, so it is faster and uses the cache better. It fits data that is naturally filtered by one attribute, such as tenant, date or region.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0107","card":"q-vector-databases-026-partitions","sec":"vector-databases-s027","q":"Which field would you partition on, and how many partitions should you create?","a":"<p>Partition by a field that most queries filter on, for example tenant id, month or region. Keep the number of partitions modest and search specific partitions. Milvus documents a maximum of 1,024 partitions per collection, and as a rule of thumb you stay far under that limit, because many partitions add overhead and fragment the data. For many tenants use a partition key instead.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0108","card":"q-vector-databases-026-partitions","sec":"vector-databases-s027","q":"In pymilvus, what is the effect of <code>partition_names</code> in this search?","a":"<p>The search only looks at the <code>tenant_1</code> partition instead of the whole collection. That is faster and it also keeps the results to that tenant's data. Without <code>partition_names</code> all partitions are searched.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>collection.search(data=[query_vector], anns_field=&quot;embedding&quot;,\n                  param={&quot;metric_type&quot;: &quot;L2&quot;}, limit=10,\n                  partition_names=[&quot;tenant_1&quot;])</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0109","card":"q-vector-databases-026-partitions","sec":"vector-databases-s027","q":"In Milvus, what is the <code>_default</code> partition?","a":"<p>Every collection is created with a <code>_default</code> partition. Inserts without a <code>partition_name</code> go there. You cannot drop it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0110","card":"q-vector-databases-026-partitions","sec":"vector-databases-s027","q":"What is the difference between manually created partitions and a partition key in Milvus?","a":"<p>With manual partitions you create each one and choose it on insert and search. With a partition key (Milvus 2.2.9 and later) you mark one field and Milvus hashes its value to route each row to an internal partition. You do not manage partitions, and a filter on that field prunes the partitions searched. It scales better to many tenants.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>tenant_id = FieldSchema(name=&quot;tenant_id&quot;, dtype=DataType.INT64, is_partition_key=True)\nschema = CollectionSchema(fields=[id_field, tenant_id, embedding_field],\n                          partition_key_field=&quot;tenant_id&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0111","card":"q-vector-databases-026-partitions","sec":"vector-databases-s027","q":"How can you load or release just one partition into memory in pymilvus, and why would you?","a":"<p>Take the partition handle with <code>collection.partition(\"tenant_1\")</code> and call <code>load()</code> or <code>release()</code> on it. This keeps only the hot data in RAM, which saves memory when the whole collection is large and most queries touch a few partitions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0112","card":"q-vector-databases-027-indexes","sec":"vector-databases-s028","q":"What is the role of an index in a vector database, and what are the types?","a":"<p>An index speeds up retrieval. A vector index (ANN) makes similarity search fast with high recall. A scalar index, like a database index, speeds up filters on string and numeric fields. Milvus allows one index per field, with no composite indexes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0113","card":"q-vector-databases-027-indexes","sec":"vector-databases-s028","q":"Which Milvus vector index would you choose for a small dataset, a large general-purpose dataset, a latency-critical dataset, and a memory-constrained dataset?","a":"<p>Small (under about 10K vectors): FLAT, brute force with exact results. Large general-purpose: IVF_FLAT, the balanced and most popular choice. Latency-critical with enough RAM: HNSW, very high recall and speed with higher memory. Memory-constrained: IVF_SQ8 or IVF_PQ, which compress vectors. With a GPU: GPU_IVF_FLAT.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0114","card":"q-vector-databases-027-indexes","sec":"vector-databases-s028","q":"In an IVF_FLAT index in Milvus, what do <code>nlist</code> and <code>nprobe</code> mean?","a":"<p><code>nlist</code> is the number of clusters the vectors are split into when building the index. <code>nprobe</code> is how many of those clusters a query searches. A higher <code>nprobe</code> improves recall and costs latency. A low value is fast but may miss neighbours that sit in unsearched clusters.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>index_params = {&quot;metric_type&quot;: &quot;L2&quot;, &quot;index_type&quot;: &quot;IVF_FLAT&quot;, &quot;params&quot;: {&quot;nlist&quot;: 1024}}\nsearch_params = {&quot;metric_type&quot;: &quot;L2&quot;, &quot;params&quot;: {&quot;nprobe&quot;: 10}}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0115","card":"q-vector-databases-027-indexes","sec":"vector-databases-s028","q":"In a Milvus HNSW index, what do <code>M</code>, <code>efConstruction</code> and <code>ef</code> control?","a":"<p><code>M</code> is the maximum number of connections per node in the graph: more means better recall and more memory. <code>efConstruction</code> is the candidate list size while building: higher gives a better graph and a slower build. <code>ef</code> is the candidate list size at search time: higher gives better recall and slower queries. <code>ef</code> must be at least the <code>limit</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{&quot;metric_type&quot;: &quot;COSINE&quot;, &quot;index_type&quot;: &quot;HNSW&quot;, &quot;params&quot;: {&quot;M&quot;: 16, &quot;efConstruction&quot;: 200}}\n{&quot;metric_type&quot;: &quot;COSINE&quot;, &quot;params&quot;: {&quot;ef&quot;: 64}}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0116","card":"q-vector-databases-027-indexes","sec":"vector-databases-s028","q":"Why must the <code>metric_type</code> in the search match the one used when the index was built?","a":"<p>The index structure is built around one distance function. Searching with another metric gives an error or wrong ranking, because the graph or clusters do not reflect that distance. Decide the metric first, then build the index.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0117","card":"q-vector-databases-027-indexes","sec":"vector-databases-s028","q":"What is a scalar index in Milvus and when would you create one?","a":"<p>It speeds up filter expressions on scalar fields. <code>TRIE</code> is for VARCHAR fields and <code>STL_SORT</code> for numeric fields. Create one on fields that appear in filters of large collections, so a filter does not scan every row. It does nothing for similarity search itself.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>collection.create_index(field_name=&quot;title&quot;, index_params={&quot;index_type&quot;: &quot;TRIE&quot;})\ncollection.create_index(field_name=&quot;course_id&quot;, index_params={&quot;index_type&quot;: &quot;STL_SORT&quot;})</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0118","card":"q-vector-databases-027-indexes","sec":"vector-databases-s028","q":"What must be done before the first vector search works on a new Milvus collection?","a":"<p>Build the vector index, wait until it is finished (<code>index_building_progress</code> shows <code>indexed_rows</code> equal to <code>total_rows</code>), then load the collection into memory with <code>load()</code>, and only then search. A search on an unloaded collection returns an error.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>collection.create_index(&quot;embedding&quot;, index_params)\nutility.index_building_progress(&quot;collection_name&quot;)\ncollection.load()\nresults = collection.search(...)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0119","card":"q-vector-databases-027-indexes","sec":"vector-databases-s028","q":"What is the trade-off between FLAT and IVF_FLAT in Milvus?","a":"<p>FLAT compares the query against every vector, giving 100% recall but latency that grows linearly with the data, so it suits small sets. IVF_FLAT searches only the nprobe nearest clusters, so it is much faster on large sets but recall is under 100%.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0120","card":"q-vector-databases-027-indexes","sec":"vector-databases-s028","q":"Why does HNSW usually use more memory than IVF_FLAT in Milvus?","a":"<p>HNSW stores the graph links between vectors (up to <code>M</code> per node per layer) in addition to the vectors themselves, and the graph must be in RAM. IVF_FLAT stores only cluster assignments and the vectors. HNSW buys better speed and recall with that memory.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0121","card":"q-vector-databases-028-7-data-operations","sec":"vector-databases-s029","q":"In Milvus, what is the typical order of operations from loading data to searching it, and why does the order matter?","a":"<p>Insert, flush, create the index, load, then query or search. Data must exist before an index can be built on it, flush makes it persistent so it is part of the index, and the collection must be loaded into memory before it can be queried. Skipping a step gives empty results or errors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0122","card":"q-vector-databases-028-7-data-operations","sec":"vector-databases-s029","q":"Why should you insert data in bulk and not one row at a time in a vector database?","a":"<p>Each insert request has overhead, and many tiny inserts create many small segments that need compaction and slow down indexing. Batched inserts amortize the overhead, and generating embeddings in batches also cuts API calls. Batches of a few thousand rows are common.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0123","card":"q-vector-databases-029-insert-is-column-wise-not-row-wise","sec":"vector-databases-s030","q":"In pymilvus, how does the data for <code>collection.insert</code> have to be shaped?","a":"<p>As a list of columns, one list per field, in the order of the schema. It is not a list of rows. Every column must have the same length, and an auto-id primary key column is left out. Another accepted form is a list of dictionaries, one per row, with the field names as keys.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>insert_data = [i_course_id, i_title, i_description, i_desc_embedding]\nmr = course_collection.insert(insert_data)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0124","card":"q-vector-databases-029-insert-is-column-wise-not-row-wise","sec":"vector-databases-s030","q":"An insert fails with a message about a mismatch between the data and the schema. What do you check?","a":"<p>That the number of columns equals the number of non-auto-id fields, that they follow the schema order, that all columns have the same length, that each vector has exactly <code>dim</code> values, that strings are within <code>max_length</code>, and that types match (for example Python ints for INT64).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0125","card":"q-vector-databases-029-insert-is-column-wise-not-row-wise","sec":"vector-databases-s030","q":"In LangChain, what is the difference between <code>embed_query</code> and <code>embed_documents</code>?","a":"<p><code>embed_query</code> embeds one text, and is intended for a search query. <code>embed_documents</code> takes a list of texts and returns a list of vectors, usually in fewer API calls. For inserting many records use <code>embed_documents</code>, which is faster and cheaper than a loop of <code>embed_query</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>i_desc_embedding = [embeddings_model.embed_query(i) for i in i_description]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0126","card":"q-vector-databases-029-insert-is-column-wise-not-row-wise","sec":"vector-databases-s030","q":"You hit a rate limit from the embeddings API while embedding many descriptions. How do you handle it?","a":"<p>Batch the texts with <code>embed_documents</code>, add a short delay or exponential backoff with retries between requests, reduce parallelism, or move to a plan with higher limits. Cache embeddings so a text is never embedded twice.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0127","card":"q-vector-databases-029-insert-is-column-wise-not-row-wise","sec":"vector-databases-s030","q":"Why should an API key never be hard-coded in a notebook that inserts data?","a":"<p>Source files and notebooks get committed and shared, and a leaked key can be abused and billed. Read it from an environment variable or a secret manager, and keep it out of version control.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0128","card":"q-vector-databases-030-flush","sec":"vector-databases-s031","q":"What is a growing segment versus a sealed segment in Milvus?","a":"<p>A segment is the unit in which Milvus stores and indexes data. New inserts go to a growing segment in memory, which is searched by brute force and has no index. When it reaches a size limit or on <code>flush</code>, it is sealed and becomes immutable, and an index can then be built on it. Searches combine the sealed, indexed segments with the growing ones, so fresh data is searchable but the growing part is slower.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0129","card":"q-vector-databases-030-flush","sec":"vector-databases-s031","q":"What is compaction in Milvus and why is it needed?","a":"<p>Compaction is a background job that merges small segments into larger ones and rewrites segments to physically remove deleted rows. It is needed because many small segments slow search and indexing, and deletes are only markers until compaction runs. It costs I/O and CPU, so Milvus runs it in the background and you rarely trigger it by hand.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0130","card":"q-vector-databases-030-flush","sec":"vector-databases-s031","q":"What does <code>flush</code> do in Milvus, and why is <code>insert</code> alone not enough for durability?","a":"<p><code>insert</code> writes to an in-memory growing segment and the write log, and Milvus persists segments to object storage asynchronously. <code>flush</code> seals the current growing segments and forces them to be written, so the data is durable and part of sealed segments that indexes can be built on. If you need guarantees after an insert, flush.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0131","card":"q-vector-databases-030-flush","sec":"vector-databases-s031","q":"Why should you not call <code>flush</code> after every single insert in Milvus?","a":"<p>Each flush seals a segment, so frequent flushes make many small segments. That adds overhead, slows indexing and search, and needs compaction. Insert in batches and flush once at the end of a load, or let Milvus flush automatically.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0132","card":"q-vector-databases-030-flush","sec":"vector-databases-s031","q":"In pymilvus, what is the <code>timeout</code> argument of <code>flush</code> for?","a":"<p>It is the maximum number of seconds to wait for the flush to finish. Large inserts can take longer than the default, so raise it (for example to 300) when it raises a timeout error.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>course_collection.flush(timeout=180)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0133","card":"q-vector-databases-031-upsert-insert-or-update-by-primary-key","sec":"vector-databases-s032","q":"What does <code>upsert</code> do in Milvus, and when do you use it?","a":"<p>For each primary key it updates the entity if the key exists and inserts it if not, so id 1 is updated and id 2 is new. Use it for idempotent loads and for refreshing documents when the source changed, for example when a text is edited and its embedding has to be recomputed.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>collection.upsert([[1, 2], [&quot;Updated Title&quot;, &quot;New Title&quot;], [[0.1, 0.2], [0.3, 0.4]]])</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0134","card":"q-vector-databases-031-upsert-insert-or-update-by-primary-key","sec":"vector-databases-s032","q":"In Milvus 2.3, how is an upsert implemented and what is the limitation?","a":"<p>In Milvus 2.3 an upsert is executed as a delete of the old entity followed by an insert of the new one, and it requires the primary key values to be supplied. It is therefore not supported on collections whose primary key uses <code>auto_id=True</code>. Check the release notes for newer versions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0135","card":"q-vector-databases-031-upsert-insert-or-update-by-primary-key","sec":"vector-databases-s032","q":"There is no update-in-place for a vector field in Milvus. How do you change one field of an existing entity?","a":"<p>Upsert the whole entity with all fields, including the vector, using the same primary key. A partial update of one field is not supported in Milvus 2.3. Read the entity first if you need to keep the other values.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0136","card":"q-vector-databases-032-delete","sec":"vector-databases-s033","q":"How do you delete entities in pymilvus?","a":"<p><code>delete</code> takes a boolean expression. The dependable form is the primary key in a list, as shown. Deleting by a filter on other scalar fields depends on the Milvus version, so check your version's documentation before relying on it. Verify the result afterwards with a query.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>collection.delete(&quot;course_id in [1001, 1002, 1003]&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0137","card":"q-vector-databases-032-delete","sec":"vector-databases-s033","q":"In Milvus, why can a deleted entity still take space for a while?","a":"<p>Deletes are logical: Milvus records delete markers and hides the data from queries. The space is reclaimed later by compaction, which rewrites segments. So deleted data does not free storage instantly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0138","card":"q-vector-databases-032-delete","sec":"vector-databases-s033","q":"What precautions do you take before running a bulk delete on a production collection?","a":"<p>Run the same filter as a <code>query</code> first to see which entities match, take a backup or export, run the delete, then query again to verify. Restrict delete permission to a few roles.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0139","card":"q-vector-databases-033-drop","sec":"vector-databases-s034","q":"What is the difference between deleting entities and dropping a collection in Milvus?","a":"<p>Delete removes selected entities and keeps the collection, schema and indexes. Dropping removes the whole collection with its data and indexes, permanently. Dropping a database requires its collections to be dropped first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0140","card":"q-vector-databases-033-drop","sec":"vector-databases-s034","q":"What should you do before dropping a collection or a database in a production Milvus deployment?","a":"<p>Back up or export the data, confirm the name and the connection alias (the right server and database), and use <code>has_collection</code> to check it first. The drop cannot be undone. Limit drop privileges to admin roles.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0141","card":"q-vector-databases-033-drop","sec":"vector-databases-s034","q":"In pymilvus, why pass <code>using=</code> when dropping a collection?","a":"<p><code>using</code> selects the connection alias, so you know which server the call acts on. With several connections a missing alias would act on the <code>default</code> connection, which may be a different environment.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>utility.drop_collection(collection_name, using=connection_id)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0142","card":"q-vector-databases-034-load-into-memory","sec":"vector-databases-s035","q":"Why must a Milvus collection be loaded before you query or search it?","a":"<p>Search and query run on index and data segments held in the memory of query nodes. <code>load()</code> reads them from object storage into RAM. An unloaded collection gives an error. The price is memory: only load what you use.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0143","card":"q-vector-databases-034-load-into-memory","sec":"vector-databases-s035","q":"What is the practical memory implication of <code>load()</code> in Milvus, and how do you control it?","a":"<p>Loading puts the whole collection (vectors and indexes) in RAM, so a collection must fit in the query nodes' memory. To control it, load only the needed partitions, release collections that are not used with <code>release()</code>, use compressed indexes such as IVF_SQ8, or add query nodes. Milvus also offers replicas to raise throughput at the cost of more memory.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>course_collection.load()</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0144","card":"q-vector-databases-034-load-into-memory","sec":"vector-databases-s035","q":"You inserted new entities after loading a collection. Are they searchable?","a":"<p>Yes, in Milvus new data goes to growing segments that are searched together with the loaded sealed segments, subject to the consistency level of the request. Flush and index build later turn them into sealed, indexed segments. You do not need to reload for each insert.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0145","card":"q-vector-databases-035-8-query-vs-search","sec":"vector-databases-s036","q":"What is the difference between <code>query</code> and <code>search</code> in Milvus?","a":"<p><code>query</code> filters scalar fields with an expression (like SQL <code>SELECT ... WHERE</code>) and returns the matching entities, with no ranking. <code>search</code> is a vector similarity search (ANN) that returns the top K nearest entities ranked by distance. Search uses a vector index, while query does not require one.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0146","card":"q-vector-databases-035-8-query-vs-search","sec":"vector-databases-s036","q":"When do you use <code>query</code> and when <code>search</code> in an application that stores documents and their embeddings?","a":"<p>Use <code>search</code> to find semantically relevant items for a text query. Use <code>query</code> to fetch records by id or scalar condition, for example to read a document back, check existence, or count items matching a filter. A search with a filter expression combines both.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0147","card":"q-vector-databases-036-query","sec":"vector-databases-s037","q":"In Milvus (pymilvus 2.x), what does this call do, and why are <code>limit</code> and <code>offset</code> used together?","a":"<p>It filters entities with a scalar expression and returns the second page of 10 rows as a list of dicts. <code>offset=10</code> skips the first 10 matches and <code>limit=10</code> returns the next 10. A query has no relevance score, so the order is not a ranking. Milvus 2.x also caps <code>offset + limit</code> (16384), so deep pagination needs a different approach, such as filtering on the last seen primary key.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>page2 = collection.query(expr=&quot;course_id &gt; 0&quot;, output_fields=[&quot;title&quot;], limit=10, offset=10)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0148","card":"q-vector-databases-036-query","sec":"vector-databases-s037","q":"In Milvus, which kinds of filter expressions can you write for <code>query</code> and <code>search</code>?","a":"<p>Boolean expressions over scalar fields:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Comparison: <code>==</code>, <code>!=</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&gt;=</code>, <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&lt;=</code>.</li>\n  <li>Membership: <code>field in [1, 2, 3]</code>.</li>\n  <li>Logic: <code>and</code>/<code>&amp;&amp;</code>, <code>or</code>/<code>||</code>, <code>not</code>.</li>\n  <li>Text: <code>like 'abc%'</code> (prefix match is the portable form; suffix and infix matching depend on the Milvus version and are slower).</li>\n  <li>JSON and array fields: <code>metadata[\"category\"] == \"AI\"</code>, <code>array_contains(tags, \"x\")</code>, <code>array_length(tags) &gt; 3</code> (JSON and ARRAY types need Milvus 2.3 or later for arrays). String literals must be quoted.</li>\n</ul>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0149","card":"q-vector-databases-036-query","sec":"vector-databases-s037","q":"In Milvus, how do you filter on a field stored inside a JSON field, and when would you use a JSON field instead of separate scalar fields?","a":"<p>Use the key access syntax in the expression, for example <code>metadata[\"category\"] == \"AI\"</code>. A JSON field suits attributes that vary per entity or change often, because no schema change is needed. The cost is weaker typing and slower filtering than a dedicated, indexed scalar field. Fields you filter on in every query should be real scalar fields.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0150","card":"q-vector-databases-036-query","sec":"vector-databases-s037","q":"Can Milvus <code>query</code> compute <code>SUM</code>, <code>AVG</code> or <code>GROUP BY</code> like SQL? What do you do when you need them?","a":"<p>No. In Milvus 2.3 a query only filters and returns entities (plus counts). For aggregations, fetch the needed rows and aggregate in application code, or keep the analytical data in an RDBMS or warehouse and use the vector database only for similarity search.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0151","card":"q-vector-databases-036-query","sec":"vector-databases-s037","q":"A service builds a Milvus filter with an f-string from user input. What is the risk and what do you do?","a":"<p>Filter injection. A user can close the quote and add conditions (for example to read other tenants' rows). Validate input against a whitelist or type, escape quotes, and never let the user supply field names or operators. Enforce tenant and permission filters on the server side and add them to the expression yourself.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>results = collection.query(expr=f&quot;title == &#x27;{user_text}&#x27;&quot;, output_fields=[&quot;title&quot;])</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0152","card":"q-vector-databases-036-query","sec":"vector-databases-s037","q":"Why does a scalar <code>query</code> in Milvus not need a vector index, and what speeds it up?","a":"<p>It evaluates a boolean expression over scalar values and does no nearest-neighbour search, so there is no ANN index to use. The collection still has to be loaded. For large collections, a scalar index (such as <code>STL_SORT</code> for numbers or <code>TRIE</code> for strings) and <code>partition_names</code> reduce the amount of data scanned.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0153","card":"q-vector-databases-037-search","sec":"vector-databases-s038","q":"In pymilvus, what are <code>ignore_growing</code> and <code>offset</code> in the search parameters, and when would you change them?","a":"<p><code>ignore_growing=False</code> (default) also searches growing segments, which hold recently inserted data that is not yet sealed and indexed, so fresh data is found. <code>True</code> skips them for lower latency but fresh rows may be missing. <code>offset</code> skips the first N hits and is used for pagination. <code>nprobe</code> inside <code>params</code> is the index-specific search setting (IVF cluster count to scan).</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>search_params = {\n    &quot;metric_type&quot;: &quot;L2&quot;,\n    &quot;offset&quot;: 0,\n    &quot;ignore_growing&quot;: False,\n    &quot;params&quot;: {&quot;nprobe&quot;: 10}\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0154","card":"q-vector-databases-037-search","sec":"vector-databases-s038","q":"In Milvus, what does <code>search</code> return when <code>data</code> holds several query vectors, and why would you send them together?","a":"<p>It returns one list of hits per query vector, in the same order (<code>results[0]</code>, <code>results[1]</code>, ...). Each hit has <code>.id</code>, <code>.distance</code> and the requested fields through <code>.entity</code>. Batching saves network round trips and lets the server process the queries together, which raises throughput when you have many users or documents to compare.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0155","card":"q-vector-databases-037-search","sec":"vector-databases-s038","q":"In pymilvus, how do you read the fields of one hit from a vector search?","a":"<p><code>s_results[0]</code> is the list of hits for the first query vector, sorted best first. <code>hit.id</code> is the primary key, <code>hit.distance</code> is the score under the index metric, and <code>hit.entity.get(\"title\")</code> reads a field only if it was listed in <code>output_fields</code>. Fields not in <code>output_fields</code> are not returned.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>for hit in s_results[0]:\n    print(hit.id, hit.distance, hit.entity.get(&quot;title&quot;))</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0156","card":"q-vector-databases-037-search","sec":"vector-databases-s038","q":"Which prerequisites must hold for a Milvus vector search to give meaningful results?","a":"<p>The collection has an index on the vector field (or you accept a brute-force search) and is loaded. The query was embedded with the same model as the stored vectors, and the search <code>metric_type</code> matches the index metric. If any of these fail, you get an error or plausible-looking but wrong results.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0157","card":"q-vector-databases-037-search","sec":"vector-databases-s038","q":"In Milvus, why is it better to request few <code>output_fields</code> and a small <code>limit</code>?","a":"<p>Every returned field has to be read from storage and sent over the network. Large text fields and a big <code>limit</code> raise latency and memory use. Return only the primary key and the fields you need, then fetch heavier data by key when it is needed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0158","card":"q-vector-databases-038-search-always-returns-results-use-a-thre","sec":"vector-databases-s039","q":"In a vector database, why does a search for an unrelated query still return results, and what do you do about it?","a":"<p>Nearest-neighbour search always returns the K closest vectors, even when none is close. There is no notion of \"no match\". Add a similarity threshold: either filter the hits by distance in code, or push it into the search with <code>radius</code> so the server drops far hits.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0159","card":"q-vector-databases-038-search-always-returns-results-use-a-thre","sec":"vector-databases-s039","q":"In Milvus, what do <code>radius</code> and <code>range_filter</code> mean for an L2 search, and how does it differ for IP and COSINE?","a":"<p>For L2 (lower is closer), <code>radius</code> is the maximum distance and <code>range_filter</code> the minimum, so hits with <code>range_filter &lt;= distance &lt; radius</code> are returned. For IP and COSINE (higher is closer) the roles flip: <code>radius</code> is the minimum score and <code>range_filter</code> the maximum. This is a range search in Milvus 2.x. It can return fewer than <code>limit</code> hits, or none.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>search_params = {&quot;metric_type&quot;: &quot;L2&quot;,\n                 &quot;params&quot;: {&quot;nprobe&quot;: 10, &quot;radius&quot;: 0.5, &quot;range_filter&quot;: 0.0}}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0160","card":"q-vector-databases-038-search-always-returns-results-use-a-thre","sec":"vector-databases-s039","q":"What is the bug when this threshold filter is reused after switching the index metric from L2 to COSINE?","a":"<p>With COSINE (or IP) a higher score is more similar, so <code>&lt; 0.5</code> keeps the least similar hits and drops the best. The comparison direction and the threshold value both depend on the metric, so store them together with the metric name and re-tune after any metric change.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>relevant = [hit for hit in s_results[0] if hit.distance &lt; 0.5]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0161","card":"q-vector-databases-038-search-always-returns-results-use-a-thre","sec":"vector-databases-s039","q":"What is the difference between filtering by distance in application code after <code>search</code> and using <code>radius</code> in the search?","a":"<p>Post-filtering takes the top-K first and then removes hits, so the result can be shorter than K and the server did the full work. <code>radius</code> makes the server apply the range during the search, so unrelated hits are never returned and less data is transferred. Post-filtering is simpler and lets you log the rejected hits while you are tuning. A range search can be a little slower on some indexes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0162","card":"q-vector-databases-038-search-always-returns-results-use-a-thre","sec":"vector-databases-s039","q":"You must choose a distance threshold for a vector search. How do you do it?","a":"<p>Do not guess a number. Build a labelled set of queries with relevant and irrelevant documents, run searches, and pick the threshold that gives the best precision/recall trade-off (for example the best F1), or the best hit ratio for the accuracy you need. Re-tune when you change the embedding model, the metric or the document types, because distances are not comparable across them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0163","card":"q-vector-databases-038-search-always-returns-results-use-a-thre","sec":"vector-databases-s039","q":"What is the weakness of choosing the threshold from each result list, for example the median distance or mean minus one standard deviation of that query's hits?","a":"<p>The threshold is relative to that query's own top-K, so it always keeps some hits and rejects others, even when every hit is irrelevant. It cannot say that nothing is relevant. An absolute threshold from a labelled set answers that.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>distances = [hit.distance for hit in s_results[0]]\nthreshold = np.mean(distances) - np.std(distances)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0164","card":"q-vector-databases-038-search-always-returns-results-use-a-thre","sec":"vector-databases-s039","q":"When a search returns nothing under the threshold, what are the options for the application?","a":"<p>Say that nothing relevant was found (best for RAG and support bots, where a wrong answer is worse than none), ask the user to rephrase, or fall back to the closest few results marked as low confidence. Falling back to the closest hits always shows something, but those hits may be irrelevant. In RAG, skip the LLM call when there is no context.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0165","card":"q-vector-databases-039-hybrid-search-vector-scalar-filter","sec":"vector-databases-s040","q":"What is hybrid search in a vector database such as Milvus, and when do you need it?","a":"<p>It combines a similarity search with a scalar filter in the <code>expr</code> argument. You need it whenever meaning is not the only requirement: category, date range, language, product, tenant, or user permission. The filter narrows the candidates and the vector search ranks them.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>results = collection.search(\n    data=[search_embed], anns_field=&quot;desc_embedding&quot;, param=search_params, limit=10,\n    expr=&quot;course_id &gt; 1000 and title like &#x27;ML%&#x27;&quot;,\n    output_fields=[&quot;title&quot;, &quot;course_id&quot;]\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0166","card":"q-vector-databases-039-hybrid-search-vector-scalar-filter","sec":"vector-databases-s040","q":"In Milvus, is the scalar filter applied before or after the vector search, and why does it matter?","a":"<p>It is applied during the search, so the engine ranks only entities that pass the filter and still returns up to <code>limit</code> hits. If you instead took the top 10 by similarity and filtered afterwards, you could end up with 0-3 results because the best matches may all fail the filter. Very selective filters can still hurt recall or speed, so index the filtered fields and consider partitions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0167","card":"q-vector-databases-039-hybrid-search-vector-scalar-filter","sec":"vector-databases-s040","q":"In Milvus, how do you paginate vector search results?","a":"<p>Use <code>offset=(page_num - 1) * page_size</code> together with <code>limit=page_size</code>. The engine still has to compute <code>offset + limit</code> candidates, so deep pages get slower, and there is a cap on <code>offset + limit</code>. For infinite scroll, a bigger <code>limit</code> cached by the application is often cheaper.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0168","card":"q-vector-databases-039-hybrid-search-vector-scalar-filter","sec":"vector-databases-s040","q":"A multi-user knowledge base must show each user only the documents they may read. How do you do this in vector search?","a":"<p>Store the access attributes (owner, group, tenant) as scalar fields and add them to the <code>expr</code> of every search, built on the server from the authenticated identity, not from client input. Do not filter after retrieval, because the top-K may be wasted on forbidden documents. For strong isolation use partition keys or one collection or database per tenant.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0169","card":"q-vector-databases-039-hybrid-search-vector-scalar-filter","sec":"vector-databases-s040","q":"In Milvus 2.4 and later, how is <code>hybrid_search</code> different from a vector search that has an <code>expr</code> filter?","a":"<p>A search with <code>expr</code> is one vector search restricted by scalar conditions. <code>hybrid_search</code> (Milvus 2.4+) runs several vector searches (for example a dense and a sparse vector field, or text and image vectors) and merges them with a reranker such as RRF or weighted scores. The word \"hybrid\" means two different things, so state which one you mean.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0170","card":"q-vector-databases-040-consistency-levels","sec":"vector-databases-s041","q":"What are the consistency levels in Milvus, and what trade-off do they make?","a":"<p>Strong, Bounded (bounded staleness), Session and Eventually.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Strong: a search sees all data written before it; the slowest because it waits.</li>\n  <li>Bounded: may be a little stale, within a time bound; a balance.</li>\n  <li>Session: a client sees its own writes; others may lag.</li>\n  <li>Eventually: no freshness guarantee, the lowest latency. The weaker the level, the less the search waits, so latency falls and freshness drops.</li>\n</ul>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0171","card":"q-vector-databases-040-consistency-levels","sec":"vector-databases-s041","q":"In Milvus, where do you set the consistency level, and how would you pick it for a semantic cache versus a product catalog search?","a":"<p>It can be set when you create the collection and overridden per request with <code>consistency_level</code> on <code>search</code> or <code>query</code>. A semantic cache wants Strong or Session, so an entry inserted a moment ago is found and the LLM is not called twice for the same prompt. A catalog that changes slowly can use Bounded or Eventually for lower latency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0172","card":"q-vector-databases-040-consistency-levels","sec":"vector-databases-s041","q":"You insert a row in Milvus and an immediate search with <code>consistency_level=\"Eventually\"</code> does not find it. Is this a bug?","a":"<p>No. Eventually consistent reads do not wait for recent writes to become visible. Use Strong (or Session in the same client) when you must read your own writes, or wait before searching. Also check that the data was inserted into a loaded collection.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0173","card":"q-vector-databases-041-troubleshooting-search","sec":"vector-databases-s042","q":"A Milvus search returns an empty list although you know matching data exists. What do you check?","a":"<p>In order:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>A <code>radius</code> threshold that is too strict for the metric, or a wrong direction for L2 versus IP/COSINE.</li>\n  <li>The <code>expr</code> filter, which may exclude everything (quoting, wrong field, type mismatch).</li>\n  <li><code>partition_names</code> or the loaded partitions.</li>\n  <li>Whether the data is visible: consistency level, and whether it was inserted into a loaded collection.</li>\n  <li>Whether the query was embedded with the same model and dimension as the data.</li>\n</ol>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0174","card":"q-vector-databases-041-troubleshooting-search","sec":"vector-databases-s042","q":"Every result of a vector search is irrelevant. What is your debugging order?","a":"<p>Print the distances first: if they are all large, the data really has no good match, so add a threshold. If the best distances look fine, check the embedding model (same model for data and query), the metric, and chunking or text cleaning. Then compare with an exact FLAT search to see whether the ANN index loses recall. Also check that vectors are normalized when the metric needs it, and that a top-K search always returns K results, so a score threshold is needed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0175","card":"q-vector-databases-041-troubleshooting-search","sec":"vector-databases-s042","q":"Why can the same ANN vector search return slightly different results between runs, and is that a bug?","a":"<p>Approximate indexes trade exactness for speed, so results depend on index parameters (<code>ef</code>, <code>nprobe</code>), the index build (graph construction, clustering), segment layout, and newly inserted data that is not yet indexed. Small differences in the lower ranks are expected. Treat it as a bug only if recall against an exact FLAT search is below your target. Raise search parameters or rebuild the index to improve recall and stability.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0176","card":"q-vector-databases-042-why","sec":"vector-databases-s043","q":"What is a semantic cache for an LLM application, and what problem does it solve?","a":"<p>It stores previous prompts, their embeddings and the LLM responses in a vector database. A new prompt is embedded and compared with the stored ones, and if one is close enough the stored response is returned without calling the LLM. It cuts cost (per-token pricing), latency (generation is slow, token by token) and load on the provider.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0177","card":"q-vector-databases-042-why","sec":"vector-databases-s043","q":"Why does a semantic cache give more hits than an exact-match cache for LLM prompts?","a":"<p>Users ask the same thing in different words (\"What is ML?\" and \"What is machine learning?\"). An exact-match key (hash of the string) misses these. Embeddings put paraphrases near each other, so a similarity search treats them as the same request.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0178","card":"q-vector-databases-042-why","sec":"vector-databases-s043","q":"What are the risks of a semantic cache, and when should you not use one?","a":"<p>False positives (a similar but different question gets a wrong cached answer), stale answers when facts change, and leaking user-specific answers to other users. Avoid or restrict it for personalised, time-sensitive or high-stakes answers, and for creative tasks where varied output is wanted. Mitigate with a tuned threshold, TTL, metadata filters (user, tenant, model) and feedback.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0179","card":"q-vector-databases-042-why","sec":"vector-databases-s043","q":"A cache hit still has to embed the prompt. Why is it still much cheaper and faster than calling the LLM?","a":"<p>One embedding call and one vector search take tens of milliseconds and cost a tiny fraction of generation. An LLM produces output token by token, so a long answer takes seconds and costs per token. The saving is largest for long answers and expensive models. The embedding call and the vector search are added to every request, hit or miss. Reduce that cost with a fast or local embedding model, an in-memory index, a service close to the database, and an exact-match cache in front for identical repeated prompts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0180","card":"q-vector-databases-043-workflow","sec":"vector-databases-s044","q":"What are the steps of a semantic cache lookup for an LLM request?","a":"<ol>\n  <li>Embed the incoming prompt. 2. Search the cache for the nearest stored prompt (top-1). 3. If its distance passes the threshold, return the stored response (hit). 4. Otherwise call the LLM, store prompt, embedding and response in the cache, and return the response (miss).</li>\n</ol>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0181","card":"q-vector-databases-043-workflow","sec":"vector-databases-s044","q":"In a semantic cache, why do you embed the prompt and not the response, and what do you store per entry?","a":"<p>Lookup compares a new prompt with old prompts, so the prompt is what must be searchable. The entry holds the prompt text, the prompt embedding (the vector field) and the response text. Optional fields: timestamp, model name, user or tenant, feedback.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0182","card":"q-vector-databases-043-workflow","sec":"vector-databases-s044","q":"Why does a semantic cache usually search with top-1?","a":"<p>Only one cached response is returned, so the nearest entry decides the hit. Retrieving more adds cost and no benefit. If you want safety, you can retrieve a few and require the best to pass the threshold and agree with a check such as a metadata filter.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0183","card":"q-vector-databases-043-workflow","sec":"vector-databases-s044","q":"A chatbot sends the whole conversation to the LLM. What goes wrong if you cache only on the latest user message?","a":"<p>The same words can mean different things in different conversations (\"and the price?\"), so the cache returns a wrong answer. Key the cache on the full effective prompt (including relevant context), or on a rewritten standalone question, and skip the cache for turns that depend on conversation state.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0184","card":"q-vector-databases-043-workflow","sec":"vector-databases-s044","q":"Two requests with the same new prompt arrive at the same time and both miss the cache. What happens, and how do you reduce it?","a":"<p>Both call the LLM and both insert, so the cache gets duplicates and you pay twice. Use a short per-prompt lock or request coalescing so one request calls the LLM and the others wait, and remove near-duplicates in periodic cleanup.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0185","card":"q-vector-databases-044-cache-collection-setup-notebook-code-04","sec":"vector-databases-s045","q":"In Milvus, why does a cache collection use an automatically assigned primary key?","a":"<p>Cache entries have no natural key; the prompt text is not unique or stable. With <code>auto_id=True</code> Milvus assigns the id on insert, so inserts leave that column out and cannot collide. The id can still be read back from a query and used in <code>delete</code> to prune entries.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>cache_id = FieldSchema(name=&quot;cache_id&quot;, dtype=DataType.INT64, auto_id=True, is_primary=True, max_length=32)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0186","card":"q-vector-databases-044-cache-collection-setup-notebook-code-04","sec":"vector-databases-s045","q":"In Milvus, what happens to the cache write when the LLM response is longer than the declared <code>max_length</code> of the VARCHAR field?","a":"<p>The insert fails with a schema/length error, because VARCHAR fields have a fixed maximum. Size the field for the longest expected response, truncate before storing, or skip caching very long responses. The limit cannot be changed on an existing field without recreating the collection.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>response_text = FieldSchema(name=&quot;response_text&quot;, dtype=DataType.VARCHAR, max_length=2048)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0187","card":"q-vector-databases-044-cache-collection-setup-notebook-code-04","sec":"vector-databases-s045","q":"Why is a cache for LLM answers often placed in its own Milvus database or collection, separate from the knowledge base in a RAG application?","a":"<p>The two have different lifecycles and schemas: cache entries are written on every miss and pruned often, while knowledge-base chunks change when documents change. Separate collections allow different indexes, thresholds, retention and access rights, and one can be dropped or tuned without touching the other.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0188","card":"q-vector-databases-045-inference-loop-notebook-code-04","sec":"vector-databases-s046","q":"In a Milvus-backed LLM cache, how can the <code>radius</code> search parameter replace an explicit distance check?","a":"<p><code>radius</code> makes Milvus return only hits within the distance limit, so any returned hit already passes the threshold. A hit is just a non-empty result list. The alternative is to search without <code>radius</code> and compare <code>hits[0].distance</code> with the threshold in code, which lets you log near-misses for tuning.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>search_params = {\n    &quot;metric_type&quot;: &quot;L2&quot;,\n    &quot;offset&quot;: 0,\n    &quot;ignore_growing&quot;: False,\n    &quot;params&quot;: {&quot;nprobe&quot;: 20, &quot;radius&quot;: similarity_threshold}\n}\n...\nif len(cache_results[0]) &gt; 0:                                 # cache hit</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0189","card":"q-vector-databases-045-inference-loop-notebook-code-04","sec":"vector-databases-s046","q":"Why should an LLM call that feeds a cache use a low or zero temperature?","a":"<p>A cached answer is returned for many future prompts, so it should be the stable, factual answer and not one random sample. With high temperature the stored answer can be an unusual variation that is then served repeatedly. Creative use cases are poor candidates for caching.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0190","card":"q-vector-databases-046-threshold-l2-distance","sec":"vector-databases-s047","q":"What happens if the distance threshold of a semantic cache is too low, and what if it is too high?","a":"<p>Too low (strict): few hits, little saving, but answers are accurate. Too high (loose): many hits, but false positives, where a different question gets a wrong cached answer. A cache must balance hit ratio against the accuracy of hits, and a wrong answer usually costs more than a missed hit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0191","card":"q-vector-databases-046-threshold-l2-distance","sec":"vector-databases-s047","q":"How do you tune the threshold of a semantic cache?","a":"<p>Build a benchmark of prompt pairs that should and should not match. For each candidate threshold, measure the hit ratio and the accuracy of the hits (percentage of cached answers that are correct), and optimise a combined score such as hit ratio times accuracy for your tolerance of wrong answers. Re-run it when the embedding model or metric changes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0192","card":"q-vector-databases-046-threshold-l2-distance","sec":"vector-databases-s047","q":"Why is accuracy computed only over cache hits and not over all prompts when you evaluate a cache threshold?","a":"<p>Misses go to the LLM, so their answers are as good as the LLM. The only new error the cache can introduce is a wrong answer on a hit. Accuracy over hits measures that risk, and hit ratio measures the benefit. The <code>if cache_hits &gt; 0</code> guard avoids a division by zero.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>accuracy = correct_responses / cache_hits if cache_hits &gt; 0 else 0\nhit_ratio = cache_hits / len(test_prompts)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0193","card":"q-vector-databases-046-threshold-l2-distance","sec":"vector-databases-s047","q":"\"How tall is an elephant?\" and \"How heavy is an elephant?\" are close in embedding space. How do you stop the cache from returning the wrong answer?","a":"<p>Embeddings capture topical similarity more than exact intent. Lower the threshold, use an embedding model fine-tuned for paraphrase or question matching, add a second check on a candidate hit (a reranker or cross-encoder, or a cheap LLM verification), and add metadata filters. Collect user feedback and remove entries flagged as wrong.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0194","card":"q-vector-databases-047-measuring-effectiveness","sec":"vector-databases-s048","q":"How do you measure whether an LLM semantic cache is worth it?","a":"<p>Track the cache hit ratio (hits divided by total requests), cost saved (hits times the cost of one LLM call, minus embedding and database cost), average latency, and the accuracy or false-positive rate of hits. A high hit ratio with many wrong answers is a failure, so quality metrics matter next to the saving.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0195","card":"q-vector-databases-047-measuring-effectiveness","sec":"vector-databases-s048","q":"A cache serves 70% of requests in 0.1 s while the LLM takes 3 s. What is the average latency, and which formula did you use?","a":"<p>Average latency = hit ratio times cache latency plus miss ratio times LLM latency = 0.7 x 0.1 + 0.3 x 3 = 0.97 s. Misses dominate the average, so the hit ratio is the lever. Percentile latency (p95) is also worth tracking, because misses stay slow.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0196","card":"q-vector-databases-047-measuring-effectiveness","sec":"vector-databases-s048","q":"Does a good cache hit ratio look the same for every application?","a":"<p>No. It depends on how repetitive the prompts are. FAQ and support bots have many repeated questions and can reach high ratios, general chat has medium, and creative or highly personalised tasks have low ratios. Compare against your own traffic, not a universal figure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0197","card":"q-vector-databases-047-measuring-effectiveness","sec":"vector-databases-s048","q":"A semantic cache has a hit ratio under 30%. What do you check?","a":"<p>Whether traffic is really repetitive; the threshold (maybe too strict); whether the same embedding model is used on write and read; whether prompts include unique tokens such as timestamps or user IDs that make them differ (normalise or strip them); and whether pruning or TTL removes entries too early.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0198","card":"q-vector-databases-048-cache-management-production","sec":"vector-databases-s049","q":"What are the pruning strategies for a semantic cache, and what are the trade-offs?","a":"<p>Age-based (TTL): simple, but may delete popular old entries. LRU (least recently used): keeps popular entries but needs a timestamp update on every hit. Size-based: predictable storage, but may drop recent or valuable entries. Hybrid (age plus size plus LRU): best results, more complex. Pick based on how stable your answers are and how large the cache can grow.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0199","card":"q-vector-databases-048-cache-management-production","sec":"vector-databases-s049","q":"Why does a semantic cache need a TTL even when the threshold is well tuned?","a":"<p>Cached answers become wrong when the world changes (prices, policies, news) or when the model or prompt template is upgraded. A TTL bounds how long a stale answer can be served. Set it by how fast the underlying facts change, and flush the cache when the model, prompt or embedding model changes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0200","card":"q-vector-databases-048-cache-management-production","sec":"vector-databases-s049","q":"Why put a <code>model_name</code> field in a shared cache, and how is it used?","a":"<p>Different models give different answers and quality. Without the field, a prompt answered by a cheap model could be served to a request that needs a stronger one. The <code>expr</code> filter restricts the nearest-neighbour search to entries from the right model. The same idea works for tenant, language or prompt version.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>results = cache.search(data=[embedding], anns_field=&quot;prompt_embedding&quot;, param={&quot;metric_type&quot;: &quot;L2&quot;},\n                       limit=1, expr=&quot;model_name == &#x27;gpt-4&#x27;&quot;, output_fields=[&quot;response_text&quot;])</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0201","card":"q-vector-databases-048-cache-management-production","sec":"vector-databases-s049","q":"What is cache warming, and why do it?","a":"<p>Pre-populating the cache with answers to known frequent or critical questions (for example FAQ) before traffic arrives. The first real users get hits at once, and you control the quality of those answers. The risk is stale content, so combine it with a TTL.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0202","card":"q-vector-databases-048-cache-management-production","sec":"vector-databases-s049","q":"How can user feedback improve a semantic cache?","a":"<p>Store a feedback field per entry (for example +1 or -1 from thumbs up or down). Delete or down-rank entries that get negative feedback, so a wrong cached answer is not repeated. It also gives labelled data for tuning the threshold.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0203","card":"q-vector-databases-048-cache-management-production","sec":"vector-databases-s049","q":"What security and privacy rules apply to a semantic cache shared by many users?","a":"<p>Do not cache responses that contain personal or tenant-specific data unless the cache key and filters include the user or tenant, otherwise one user can receive another's answer. Apply the same access control as the original data, set retention limits, and treat the cache as sensitive storage.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0204","card":"q-vector-databases-049-cache-management-production","sec":"vector-databases-s049","q":"What does this age-based prune do, and what must you check before running a delete like it in production?","a":"<p>It deletes every entity whose timestamp is older than seven days. Check that the field exists and holds epoch seconds in the same unit, that the clock source is consistent, and test the expression with a <code>query</code> first to see how many rows it matches. Some Milvus versions only accept primary-key expressions in <code>delete</code>; the safe pattern is to query the ids and delete by <code>id in [...]</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>one_week_ago = int(time.time()) - (7 * 24 * 60 * 60)\ncache.delete(f&quot;timestamp &lt; {one_week_ago}&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0205","card":"q-vector-databases-049-cache-management-production","sec":"vector-databases-s049","q":"Why does this LRU prune step not remove the least recently used entries?","a":"<p><code>query</code> is a filter, not a sorted read. Without ordering it returns an arbitrary set of matching rows, so <code>limit</code> picks arbitrary rows. To find the oldest entries, filter by a timestamp range (for example <code>last_used &lt; cutoff</code>) or fetch the timestamps and sort in application code, then delete by id. Do not assume that a query supports <code>ORDER BY</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>old_entries = cache_collection.query(expr=&quot;cache_id &gt; 0&quot;, output_fields=[&quot;cache_id&quot;, &quot;last_used&quot;],\n                                     limit=current_size - max_size)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0206","card":"q-vector-databases-049-cache-management-production","sec":"vector-databases-s049","q":"In a Milvus-backed cache, why keep a <code>last_used</code> field and refresh it with an upsert on every hit, and what is the downside?","a":"<p><code>last_used</code> lets you prune the least recently used entries, which gives exact LRU. Milvus has no partial update, so the call must send the whole entity, including the 1536-float vector, and every read becomes a full write that later needs compaction. Use it for small caches, or approximate the timestamp with batched or sampled updates, or update only when the stored time is older than some interval.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>cache_collection.upsert([[hit_id], [prompt_text], [response_text], [embedding], [int(time.time())]])</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0207","card":"q-vector-databases-049-cache-management-production","sec":"vector-databases-s049","q":"Why wrap cache logic in a class that counts hits and misses?","a":"<p>The counters give the hit ratio and cost saving, which are needed to tune the threshold and to prove the cache is worth running. A wrapper also keeps the threshold, embedding model and LLM together, so they cannot drift apart, and gives one place to add logging, locking and TTL.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0208","card":"q-vector-databases-050-llm-capabilities-and-limits","sec":"vector-databases-s050","q":"What are the limitations of an LLM as a source of knowledge for an enterprise application?","a":"<p>It knows only its training data (with a cut-off date, so recent facts are missing), it has no access to private company data, it can hallucinate (confident but false answers), it cannot cite where an answer came from, and updating its knowledge by retraining is expensive. These limits are why RAG is used.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0209","card":"q-vector-databases-050-llm-capabilities-and-limits","sec":"vector-databases-s050","q":"What is a hallucination in an LLM, why does it happen, and how do you reduce it?","a":"<p>The model produces fluent text that is false or unsupported. It happens because the model predicts likely tokens, not verified facts, and fills gaps with plausible text. Reduce it by grounding the answer in retrieved documents (RAG), instructing the model to answer only from context and say \"I don't know\", lowering temperature, asking for citations, and validating outputs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0210","card":"q-vector-databases-050-llm-capabilities-and-limits","sec":"vector-databases-s050","q":"An LLM answers questions about your company's internal policies wrongly. What are your options to fix it?","a":"<p>Give it the policy text at query time (RAG), or fine-tune it on the policies. RAG is the usual first choice: it is cheaper, updates when documents change, can cite documents and respects access control. Fine-tuning changes style or behaviour but is poor at injecting facts that change often.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0211","card":"q-vector-databases-050-llm-capabilities-and-limits","sec":"vector-databases-s050","q":"What are an LLM's two capabilities, and which one does RAG rely on?","a":"<p>Language (understanding, generating, translating, holding a conversation) and knowledge (facts learned in training). RAG keeps the language skill and replaces the knowledge part with retrieved company data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0212","card":"q-vector-databases-051-what-rag-is","sec":"vector-databases-s051","q":"What is Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), and what problem does it solve?","a":"<p>RAG retrieves relevant documents from a knowledge base and adds them to the prompt, so a standard LLM answers from that context. It gives the model current and private knowledge, reduces hallucination because the answer is grounded, and allows citations, without retraining.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0213","card":"q-vector-databases-051-what-rag-is","sec":"vector-databases-s051","q":"What does each word in Retrieval-Augmented Generation do?","a":"<p>Retrieval: find the most relevant chunks for the question (usually by vector similarity, often with scalar filters). Augmented: put those chunks in the prompt with instructions. Generation: the LLM writes the answer using the context.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0214","card":"q-vector-databases-051-what-rag-is","sec":"vector-databases-s051","q":"Does RAG remove hallucinations completely?","a":"<p>No. It reduces them. The model can still ignore the context, misread it, or answer from its training data when retrieval returned nothing useful. Retrieval can also miss the right chunk. Counter with strict prompt instructions, a threshold on retrieved chunks, citations, and evaluation of faithfulness.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0215","card":"q-vector-databases-051-what-rag-is","sec":"vector-databases-s051","q":"What are the drawbacks and limits of RAG?","a":"<p>Answer quality depends on retrieval quality (chunking, embeddings, filters). It adds latency (embedding, search, longer prompt) and cost for the larger prompt. The context window limits how much can be retrieved, it is weak at questions needing reasoning over the whole corpus (for example summarising everything), and it needs a pipeline to keep documents fresh.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0216","card":"q-vector-databases-051-what-rag-is","sec":"vector-databases-s051","q":"When would you use RAG and when would you put all documents straight into a long-context LLM prompt?","a":"<p>Put everything in the prompt when the corpus is small, a single or a few documents, and the questions need reasoning across the whole text. Use RAG when the corpus is large or changes, when cost and latency matter (a long prompt is paid for on every call), when you need per-user access control, or when you want citations. Long contexts also lose accuracy for facts buried in the middle. The two can be combined: retrieve a generous set of chunks and rely on a larger window.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0217","card":"q-vector-databases-051-what-rag-is","sec":"vector-databases-s051","q":"Why is a vector database a good fit for the retrieval step of RAG?","a":"<p>It finds chunks that are close in meaning to the question, not only those with the same words, and it scales to millions of chunks with ANN indexes. It also combines the search with scalar filters (product, date, permissions) and supports updates as documents change.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0218","card":"q-vector-databases-051-what-rag-is","sec":"vector-databases-s051","q":"In RAG, why do you use scalar filters together with vector similarity?","a":"<p>The most similar chunk may belong to the wrong product, an old version or a document the user must not see. Filters on metadata (product, date, language, access) restrict retrieval to valid candidates, improving precision and enforcing permissions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0219","card":"q-vector-databases-052-rag-vs-custom-llm-figures-are-the-course","sec":"vector-databases-s052","q":"When do you choose RAG, fine-tuning, or training a model from scratch?","a":"<p>RAG when the need is knowledge: private, changing or citeable data. Fine-tuning when the need is behaviour: a style, format, domain vocabulary or task skill. Training from scratch only for very large organisations with unique data and a strong reason. RAG is the cheapest and fastest and updates by adding documents. Often RAG and fine-tuning are combined.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0220","card":"q-vector-databases-052-rag-vs-custom-llm-figures-are-the-course","sec":"vector-databases-s052","q":"Why is updating knowledge easier with RAG than with a fine-tuned or custom LLM?","a":"<p>A new or changed document is chunked, embedded and upserted into the vector database, and is searchable minutes later. A fine-tuned or custom model must be retrained, which costs compute, time and testing, and old facts may stay in the weights.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0221","card":"q-vector-databases-052-rag-vs-custom-llm-figures-are-the-course","sec":"vector-databases-s052","q":"How does RAG help with access control and compliance compared with fine-tuning?","a":"<p>With RAG, the data stays in a store you control, and each query can filter by the user's permissions, so users only get content they may read. Deleting a document removes it from the answers. With fine-tuning, the data is baked into the weights and cannot be restricted per user or removed easily.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0222","card":"q-vector-databases-052-rag-vs-custom-llm-figures-are-the-course","sec":"vector-databases-s052","q":"Which running costs does a RAG system have?","a":"<p>The vector database (hosting or managed service), the embedding calls for documents and for every query, the LLM calls (the prompt grows with the retrieved context), storage, and the engineering to keep the pipeline and data fresh.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0223","card":"q-vector-databases-053-two-processes","sec":"vector-databases-s053","q":"What are the two processes of a RAG system, and why are they kept separate?","a":"<p>Knowledge curation (offline or incremental): collect, clean, chunk, embed and store documents. Question answering (online, per request): embed the question, retrieve, build the prompt and call the LLM. They have different load, latency needs and failure modes: curation can be batched and retried, answering must be fast. Separating them lets you scale and deploy each on its own.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0224","card":"q-vector-databases-053-two-processes","sec":"vector-databases-s053","q":"How do you keep a RAG knowledge base up to date as source documents change?","a":"<p>Do an initial load, then detect changes (modified time, content hash, webhooks or change feeds) and re-process only new or changed documents. Upsert chunks with stable ids or delete old chunks of a changed document, and delete chunks of removed documents. Track document version and embedding model per chunk.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0225","card":"q-vector-databases-054-knowledge-curation-pipeline","sec":"vector-databases-s054","q":"What are the stages of a RAG knowledge curation pipeline?","a":"<p>Acquisition and filtering (fetch and clean input from files, databases, tickets, web pages), standardisation (common structure, text plus metadata), chunking, embedding, and storing chunks with their vectors and metadata in the vector database. Then flush or index so they are searchable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0226","card":"q-vector-databases-054-knowledge-curation-pipeline","sec":"vector-databases-s054","q":"Why remove duplicates and personal data before embedding documents in a RAG pipeline?","a":"<p>Duplicate chunks fill the top-K results with the same text and waste storage and index memory. Personal or confidential data that is embedded can be retrieved and shown to users who should not see it, and it is hard to delete later because it is spread over chunks and vectors. Deduplicate, then redact or exclude sensitive data, before embedding, and keep the document id in metadata so you can delete all its chunks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0227","card":"q-vector-databases-054-knowledge-curation-pipeline","sec":"vector-databases-s054","q":"Why do you split documents into chunks before embedding in a RAG pipeline?","a":"<p>Embedding models have an input limit, and one vector for a long document blurs several topics. Small chunks give precise matches, so the retrieved context is relevant and the prompt stays within the LLM's context window.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0228","card":"q-vector-databases-054-knowledge-curation-pipeline","sec":"vector-databases-s054","q":"How do you choose chunk size and overlap for RAG?","a":"<p>Small chunks (a few hundred tokens) are precise but may lose context. Large chunks keep context but dilute the embedding and cost more prompt tokens. Pick by content type (short FAQ answers vs long technical docs), the embedding model's limit, and test retrieval quality on real questions. Overlap (often 10-20% of the chunk) keeps sentences that cross a boundary retrievable, at the cost of duplicate storage.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0229","card":"q-vector-databases-054-knowledge-curation-pipeline","sec":"vector-databases-s054","q":"Which chunking strategies exist for RAG, and when would you use each?","a":"<p>Fixed-size: split every N tokens or characters; simple and predictable, but can cut sentences. Recursive: try paragraph, line and sentence separators in order, so chunks end at natural boundaries; a good default for prose. Structure-aware: split by headings, sections, code blocks or table rows, which suits Markdown, HTML, manuals and code. Semantic: start a new chunk where the meaning shifts, measured by embedding similarity between sentences; better coherence at higher cost. Parent-document retrieval: embed small chunks for precise matching, then return the larger parent section to the LLM for context.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0230","card":"q-vector-databases-054-knowledge-curation-pipeline","sec":"vector-databases-s054","q":"Why keep metadata such as a document id, source type or date with each chunk?","a":"<p>It enables filtered retrieval, permissions, citations, deletion or update of all chunks of one document, and debugging. Without a document id you cannot update or remove a changed document reliably.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0231","card":"q-vector-databases-054-knowledge-curation-pipeline","sec":"vector-databases-s054","q":"How many chunks does this function return for a 2500 character text, and what is the weakness of splitting this way?","a":"<p>Three chunks: 0-1024, 924-1948 and 1848-2500. The loop moves forward by <code>chunk_size - overlap</code>. The weakness is that it cuts by characters, so it can break words and sentences. It also never terminates if <code>overlap &gt;= chunk_size</code>. A splitter that respects paragraphs and sentence boundaries is better.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def chunk_text(text, chunk_size=1024, overlap=100):\n    chunks, start = [], 0\n    while start &lt; len(text):\n        end = start + chunk_size\n        chunks.append(text[start:end])\n        start = end - overlap          # overlap for context\n    return chunks</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0232","card":"q-vector-databases-054-knowledge-curation-pipeline","sec":"vector-databases-s054","q":"Why use <code>upsert</code> and not <code>insert</code> when loading chunks into the vector database in a pipeline that re-runs?","a":"<p>Re-running <code>insert</code> with the same documents adds duplicates, which waste space and fill the top-K with identical chunks. <code>upsert</code> by a stable chunk id (for example document id plus chunk number) replaces the old chunk, so reruns and document updates are idempotent. If the number of chunks of a document shrinks, delete the leftover old chunks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0233","card":"q-vector-databases-054-knowledge-curation-pipeline","sec":"vector-databases-s054","q":"What is an ensemble knowledge base in RAG, and what does each part contribute?","a":"<p>It combines a vector database (semantic similarity over unstructured text), an RDBMS (exact structured queries and joins) and a knowledge graph such as Neo4j (relationships between entities). The system routes a question to the right store or merges answers, which handles questions that a single vector search cannot.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0234","card":"q-vector-databases-054-knowledge-curation-pipeline","sec":"vector-databases-s054","q":"What content should be left out of a RAG knowledge base?","a":"<p>Outdated, duplicate, irrelevant or low-quality content, and anything that users must not see unless access control is enforced at retrieval. Bad content gets retrieved and then used by the LLM as truth.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0235","card":"q-vector-databases-054-knowledge-curation-pipeline","sec":"vector-databases-s054","q":"In a standardisation step, why separate structured fields, text and metadata?","a":"<p>Each goes to the right place: text is embedded as the vector field, structured values become scalar fields that can be filtered and indexed, and metadata supports filtering, citation and updates. A single blob of text loses these abilities.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0236","card":"q-vector-databases-055-question-answering-pipeline","sec":"vector-databases-s055","q":"What are the steps of the question-answering pipeline in RAG, in order?","a":"<p>Validate the user and read their metadata, embed the question with the same model used for documents, search the knowledge base for top-K chunks (with scalar filters), build the context from the chunks, augment the prompt with instructions, call the LLM, and return the answer with citations.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0237","card":"q-vector-databases-055-question-answering-pipeline","sec":"vector-databases-s055","q":"How do you choose the top-K for RAG retrieval?","a":"<p>Start with a few chunks (3 to 5), then tune against an evaluation set. A small K may miss needed context, a large K adds noise, costs tokens, can exceed the context window and hurts answer quality (the model can lose relevant facts in the middle of long context). A reranker lets you retrieve more candidates and keep the best few.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0238","card":"q-vector-databases-055-question-answering-pipeline","sec":"vector-databases-s055","q":"What does a reranker do in a RAG pipeline, and how does a cross-encoder differ from the bi-encoder used for embeddings?","a":"<p>A reranker re-scores the top candidates from the vector search and reorders them, so you can retrieve many (for example 20-50) and pass only the best few to the LLM. A bi-encoder embeds the query and each chunk separately, so chunk vectors are precomputed and search is fast, but the match is coarse. A cross-encoder reads the query and one chunk together, which is more accurate but must run once per candidate, so it is too slow for the whole corpus. The cost is extra latency and a second model to run.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0239","card":"q-vector-databases-055-question-answering-pipeline","sec":"vector-databases-s055","q":"What instructions should a RAG prompt contain, and why?","a":"<p>Answer only from the context (to ground the answer and limit hallucination), say so when the context has no answer (so the model does not invent), be concise, and cite parts of the context (so users can verify). The context and the question are placed in clearly separated sections.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>augmented_prompt = f&quot;&quot;&quot;\nContext Information:\n{context}\n\nQuestion: {user_prompt}\n\nInstructions:\n...\n- If the context doesn&#x27;t contain relevant information, say &quot;I don&#x27;t have enough information to answer this question&quot;\n- Be concise and accurate\n- Cite specific parts of the context when possible\n\nAnswer:\n&quot;&quot;&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0240","card":"q-vector-databases-055-question-answering-pipeline","sec":"vector-databases-s055","q":"What is prompt injection in RAG, and how do you reduce the risk?","a":"<p>Retrieved documents can contain instructions (\"ignore previous instructions...\") that the LLM may follow. Treat retrieved text as untrusted data: separate it from instructions, tell the model not to follow instructions in the context, sanitise or limit untrusted origins, restrict tools and permissions, and filter outputs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0241","card":"q-vector-databases-055-question-answering-pipeline","sec":"vector-databases-s055","q":"In a RAG retrieval against Milvus, why is the context built from <code>results[0]</code> and why must <code>output_fields</code> include the text?","a":"<p><code>search</code> returns one hit list per query vector, and only one vector was sent, so <code>results[0]</code> holds its top 3 chunks. A hit carries only the id and distance unless a field is listed in <code>output_fields</code>, so <code>chunk_text</code> must be requested to read it, and <code>metadata</code> is returned for citations. Joining the texts with blank lines gives the LLM clearly separated passages. The <code>expr</code> restricts the search to one product.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>results = knowledge_base.search(\n    data=[prompt_embedding],\n    anns_field=&quot;chunk_embedding&quot;,\n    param={&quot;metric_type&quot;: &quot;L2&quot;, &quot;params&quot;: {&quot;nprobe&quot;: 10}},\n    limit=3,\n    expr=f&quot;product_id == &#x27;{filters[&#x27;product_id&#x27;]}&#x27;&quot;,\n    output_fields=[&quot;chunk_text&quot;, &quot;metadata&quot;]\n)\ncontext = &quot;\\n\\n&quot;.join(hit.entity.get(&quot;chunk_text&quot;) for hit in results[0])</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0242","card":"q-vector-databases-055-question-answering-pipeline","sec":"vector-databases-s055","q":"A RAG answer includes its citations. Why, and how do you provide them?","a":"<p>Citations let users verify the answer and build trust, and help debugging. Return metadata of the retrieved chunks (document title, id, page, URL) next to the answer, and ask the model to reference them. Check that cited items were in the context.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0243","card":"q-vector-databases-055-question-answering-pipeline","sec":"vector-databases-s055","q":"Where does the context go in the prompt, and how do you stay within the context window?","a":"<p>Put instructions first, then the labelled context, then the question and answer cue. Count tokens: limit chunk size and K, drop low-score chunks, trim or summarise, and keep room for the answer. Long contexts also cost more and can reduce accuracy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0244","card":"q-vector-databases-056-rag-applications","sec":"vector-databases-s056","q":"What are typical applications of RAG?","a":"<p>Chatbots over product or support documentation, automated email replies, enterprise help desks (HR, IT, legal), search and Q&amp;A over large document stores, e-commerce semantic product search and recommendation, and root-cause analysis over logs, manuals and past incidents.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0245","card":"q-vector-databases-056-rag-applications","sec":"vector-databases-s056","q":"How would you build an internal help desk bot that answers HR and IT questions from policy documents?","a":"<p>Ingest policies with metadata (department, region, version, access), chunk and embed them, store in a vector database. At question time authenticate the user, retrieve with filters for their region and permissions, build a prompt that answers only from the context and cites the policy, and add a fallback to a human when nothing relevant is found. Add feedback and evaluation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0246","card":"q-vector-databases-056-rag-applications","sec":"vector-databases-s056","q":"When is RAG a poor fit?","a":"<p>When the answer needs reasoning over the entire corpus (for example \"summarise all contracts\"), when the knowledge is small enough to put directly in the prompt, when the task is about style or behaviour (fine-tuning), or when data is highly structured and a SQL query gives exact answers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0247","card":"q-vector-databases-057-quality-metrics-and-troubleshooting","sec":"vector-databases-s057","q":"How do you evaluate a RAG system, and which metrics apply to each part?","a":"<p>Evaluate retrieval and generation separately. Retrieval: precision@k, recall@k, hit rate, MRR, latency. Generation: faithfulness (is the answer supported by the context), answer relevance, completeness, and user satisfaction. Frameworks such as RAGAS (faithfulness, answer relevancy, context precision, context recall) and LLM-as-judge scoring help, with a labelled test set of questions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0248","card":"q-vector-databases-057-quality-metrics-and-troubleshooting","sec":"vector-databases-s057","q":"A RAG answer is wrong. How do you tell whether retrieval or generation is at fault?","a":"<p>Look at the retrieved chunks for that question. If the right information is not in them, retrieval failed: check chunking, embedding model, filters, top-K and threshold. If it is there but the answer is wrong, generation failed: improve the prompt, lower temperature, reduce noise, or use a stronger model. Log the question, chunks and answer to make this possible.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0249","card":"q-vector-databases-057-quality-metrics-and-troubleshooting","sec":"vector-databases-s057","q":"RAG answers are slow. Which parts do you look at?","a":"<p>Time each stage: embedding the question, vector search (tune <code>nprobe</code> or <code>ef</code>, index type, filters), reranking, prompt size and LLM generation (the biggest part, usually). Reduce K and chunk size, cache frequent questions with a semantic cache, use a faster embedding or LLM model, stream the answer, and run steps in parallel where possible.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0250","card":"q-vector-databases-057-quality-metrics-and-troubleshooting","sec":"vector-databases-s057","q":"Retrieval brings irrelevant chunks into RAG. What can you change?","a":"<p>Better chunking (size, overlap, structure aware), scalar filters, a similarity threshold, a better or domain-tuned embedding model, cleaner source data, hybrid search with keywords (BM25), query rewriting, and a reranker on the top candidates.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0251","card":"q-vector-databases-057-quality-metrics-and-troubleshooting","sec":"vector-databases-s057","q":"Why is a RAG evaluation set needed, and how do you build it?","a":"<p>Without it you cannot tell whether a change to chunking, embeddings or prompts helped. Collect real or realistic questions with expected answers and the documents that hold them, then run it after every change. Keep it up to date as the knowledge base changes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0252","card":"q-vector-databases-058-11-rag-implementation-with-milvus","sec":"vector-databases-s058","q":"In LangChain, why is <code>RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter</code> usually preferred to a plain fixed-size splitter, and what do its parameters do?","a":"<p>It tries separators in order (paragraph, line, sentence or space, then characters), so chunks end at natural boundaries where possible and are cut mid-text only as a last resort. <code>chunk_size</code> is the maximum chunk length, <code>chunk_overlap</code> repeats text at the boundary, and <code>length_function</code> defines how length is measured (characters here; a token counter is more accurate for embedding limits). In recent LangChain versions the splitter lives in the <code>langchain_text_splitters</code> package.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>text_splitter = RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter(\n    chunk_size=512,          # characters\n    chunk_overlap=32,        # allowed overlap across chunks\n    length_function=len      # measure size in characters\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0253","card":"q-vector-databases-058-11-rag-implementation-with-milvus","sec":"vector-databases-s058","q":"In a Milvus RAG collection, why can the chunk primary key be assigned manually and not automatically assigned?","a":"<p>Manual ids let you control and reproduce them: a deterministic id (for example from document id and chunk number) makes <code>upsert</code> idempotent and lets you update or delete all chunks of a document. With <code>auto_id=True</code> every re-run creates new ids and duplicates. The caveat is that simple counters collide across runs, so use stable ids from the document.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>chunk_id_field = FieldSchema(name=&quot;chunk_id&quot;, dtype=DataType.INT64, is_primary=True, max_length=32)\nrecord_ids = [i for i in range(len(rag_text))]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0254","card":"q-vector-databases-058-11-rag-implementation-with-milvus","sec":"vector-databases-s058","q":"Which loader do you pick for PDFs in LangChain, and what should you check about the extracted text?","a":"<p>Options include <code>PDFMinerLoader</code>, <code>PyPDFLoader</code> and <code>PyMuPDFLoader</code>; they differ in speed and in how they handle layout, tables and scans. Check the extracted text for broken lines, headers and footers repeated on every page, and tables. Scanned PDFs need OCR. Bad extraction produces bad chunks, whatever the model.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0255","card":"q-vector-databases-058-11-rag-implementation-with-milvus","sec":"vector-databases-s058","q":"In a Milvus RAG schema, what must the <code>max_length</code> of the text field respect, and how do you set it with the chunk size?","a":"<p><code>max_length</code> on the VARCHAR field must be at least as long as the largest chunk you store, otherwise the insert fails, so set the chunk size and the field length together. (<code>max_length</code> applies only to VARCHAR fields; it has no effect on the INT64 key.)</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>rag_text_field = FieldSchema(name=&quot;rag_text&quot;, dtype=DataType.VARCHAR, max_length=2048)\nrag_embedding_field = FieldSchema(name=&quot;rag_embedding&quot;, dtype=DataType.FLOAT_VECTOR, dim=1536)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0256","card":"q-vector-databases-059-11-rag-implementation-with-milvus","sec":"vector-databases-s058","q":"What is the weakness of building the prompt like this, and how would you improve it?","a":"<p><code>str(context)</code> prints a Python list with brackets, quotes and escaped newlines, which is noisy for the model. Join the chunks with separators, label them (and their origin), put instructions on how to answer and what to do if the context lacks the answer, and keep the question clearly separate from the context.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>context = [results[0][i].entity.get(&quot;rag_text&quot;) for i in range(len(results[0]))]\n...\n          + &quot; Context: &quot; + str(context)\n          + &quot;\\n\\n Query: &quot; + query)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0257","card":"q-vector-databases-059-11-rag-implementation-with-milvus","sec":"vector-databases-s058","q":"In a RAG build with Milvus, why is an IVF index created after the bulk insert, and how do you check progress?","a":"<p>IVF learns its clusters from the data, so building the index once on the full bulk load gives better clusters and is cheaper than rebuilding as data trickles in. <code>index_building_progress</code> shows total, indexed and pending rows, so you know when search will be fast.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>mr = i_collection.insert(insert_data)\ni_collection.flush()\nindex_params = {&quot;metric_type&quot;: &quot;L2&quot;, &quot;index_type&quot;: &quot;IVF_FLAT&quot;, &quot;params&quot;: {&quot;nlist&quot;: 1024}}\ni_collection.create_index(field_name=&quot;rag_embedding&quot;, index_params=index_params)\nutility.index_building_progress(collection_name, using=conn_name)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0258","card":"q-vector-databases-059-11-rag-implementation-with-milvus","sec":"vector-databases-s058","q":"In a Milvus RAG pipeline, why combine several retrieved chunks and not pass only the top-1?","a":"<p>An answer often spans several passages, and the best-ranked chunk may be incomplete or slightly off. Passing the top few gives more complete context, at the cost of more tokens and more noise. Tune K on an evaluation set.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0259","card":"q-vector-databases-059-11-rag-implementation-with-milvus","sec":"vector-databases-s058","q":"Why use a temperature of 0 in the generation step of a RAG pipeline?","a":"<p>With 0 the model picks the most likely tokens, so answers are deterministic and stay closer to the context, which suits factual Q&amp;A. The model name here is a retired legacy completion model; newer code uses chat models through a current client.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>llm = OpenAI(temperature=0., model=&quot;text-davinci-003&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0260","card":"q-vector-databases-060-choosing-a-vector-database","sec":"vector-databases-s059","q":"What questions decide which vector database you pick for a project?","a":"<p>Do you need persistence and durability? How much data and how many queries (single node or cluster, scaling)? How often do you combine vector search with filters, joins or aggregations? Is the data confidential (on-premise or cloud allowed)? Do you have the team to operate it (self-hosted or managed)? Also: latency target, cost, ecosystem and client libraries.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0261","card":"q-vector-databases-060-choosing-a-vector-database","sec":"vector-databases-s059","q":"How does the choice between a managed cloud vector database and a self-hosted one change the way you connect?","a":"<p>A managed cluster is reached through a URI (HTTPS endpoint) and an API token, and the vendor runs scaling, upgrades and backups. A self-hosted server is reached by host and port with credentials you manage, and you own deployment, security, monitoring and upgrades. Keep tokens in environment variables or a secret manager.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>connections.add_connection(cloud_conn={&quot;uri&quot;: &quot;https://your-cluster.vectordb.com&quot;, &quot;token&quot;: &quot;your-token&quot;})\nconnections.add_connection(local_conn={&quot;host&quot;: &quot;localhost&quot;, &quot;port&quot;: &quot;19530&quot;,\n                                       &quot;username&quot;: &quot;username&quot;, &quot;password&quot;: &quot;password&quot;})</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0262","card":"q-vector-databases-060-choosing-a-vector-database","sec":"vector-databases-s059","q":"Your company must keep documents on-premise for compliance. Which vector database options remain, and what do you take care of?","a":"<p>Self-hosted open-source options such as Milvus, Qdrant, Weaviate or pgvector, deployed in your network. You then handle encryption at rest and in transit, authentication and roles, backups, upgrades and capacity yourself. Embedding with a hosted API sends text out, so use a local embedding model if the text cannot leave.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0263","card":"q-vector-databases-061-combining-vector-and-scalar-data","sec":"vector-databases-s060","q":"What are the two ways to combine vector and scalar data in a search application, and how do you choose?","a":"<p>Single store: keep scalar fields in the vector database and run a filtered vector search; it is simple, consistent and fast for common filters. Separate stores: search vectors first, then fetch details by id from an RDBMS; it suits rich relational data, joins and aggregations, but needs synchronisation and two calls. Prefer the single store unless you need relational features or the scalar data is large or owned elsewhere.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0264","card":"q-vector-databases-061-combining-vector-and-scalar-data","sec":"vector-databases-s060","q":"In a RAG search application that keeps vectors in a vector database and metadata in an RDBMS, what are the pitfalls of this separate-store approach?","a":"<p>The filter in the RDBMS is applied after the vector search, so many of the 100 hits can be filtered out and fewer than needed remain. <code>IN</code> does not preserve similarity order, so you must re-sort by the vector score. Two stores can drift apart (a deleted row still in the vector index). Use parameterised queries and not string building (ids are integers here, which limits the injection risk).</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>vector_results = vector_collection.search(data=[embedding], anns_field=&quot;embedding&quot;, limit=100)\nids = [result.id for result in vector_results[0]]\nscalar_query = f&quot;SELECT * FROM metadata WHERE id IN ({&#x27;,&#x27;.join(map(str, ids))})&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0265","card":"q-vector-databases-061-combining-vector-and-scalar-data","sec":"vector-databases-s060","q":"Why define scalar fields such as category and timestamp in the Milvus schema and not hide them inside the text?","a":"<p>A scalar field can be filtered with exact conditions (<code>category == 'technical' and timestamp &gt; ...</code>) and indexed, so the filter is precise and fast. Text in the embedding only influences similarity and cannot guarantee a category or date range.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0266","card":"q-vector-databases-062-distance-measure-tuning","sec":"vector-databases-s061","q":"Why can you not compare distance values across metrics when tuning them?","a":"<p>The scales differ: L2 is 0 to infinity (lower is closer), COSINE is -1 to 1 and IP is unbounded (higher is closer). Compare the ranking quality (recall, precision on a labelled set), not raw numbers, and set the threshold per metric.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0267","card":"q-vector-databases-062-distance-measure-tuning","sec":"vector-databases-s061","q":"What is the problem with this loop that creates an index for each metric on one field?","a":"<p>A vector field holds one index at a time. Creating an index with different parameters on a field that is already indexed fails in Milvus, so you must release the collection and drop the index first, or use a separate collection or field per metric. After each change, rebuild and rerun the evaluation before comparing.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>for metric_name in [&quot;L2&quot;, &quot;IP&quot;, &quot;COSINE&quot;]:\n    index_params = {&quot;metric_type&quot;: metric_name, &quot;index_type&quot;: &quot;IVF_FLAT&quot;, &quot;params&quot;: {&quot;nlist&quot;: 1024}}\n    collection.create_index(&quot;embedding&quot;, index_params)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0268","card":"q-vector-databases-063-performance-tuning","sec":"vector-databases-s062","q":"How do you choose an embedding model for a retrieval application?","a":"<p>Build a test set of representative queries with the documents that should be returned, run each candidate model through the same pipeline, and compare retrieval metrics (recall@k, MRR, accuracy). Also weigh dimension (storage and speed), latency and cost, language and domain coverage, input length limit and licence. Public benchmark scores such as MTEB are a starting point only.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0269","card":"q-vector-databases-063-performance-tuning","sec":"vector-databases-s062","q":"Why optimise the distance threshold with F1, and what does F1 combine?","a":"<p>Precision is the share of returned results that are relevant, recall is the share of relevant documents that were returned. A looser threshold raises recall and lowers precision, a stricter one does the opposite. F1 is the harmonic mean, so it is high only when both are, which gives a balanced threshold. If the denominator can be 0 (both zero), guard against division by zero. Use an F-beta score if one side matters more.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>f1_score = 2 * (avg_precision * avg_recall) / (avg_precision + avg_recall)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0270","card":"q-vector-databases-063-performance-tuning","sec":"vector-databases-s062","q":"In a Milvus <code>IVF_PQ</code> index, what do <code>m</code> and <code>nbits</code> control?","a":"<p>Product quantisation splits each vector into <code>m</code> sub-vectors, and each sub-vector is encoded with <code>nbits</code> bits (a codebook of 2^nbits centroids). The stored size per vector is about <code>m * nbits / 8</code> bytes. In Milvus, <code>dim</code> must be divisible by <code>m</code>. More sub-vectors or bits improve accuracy and use more memory.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0271","card":"q-vector-databases-063-performance-tuning","sec":"vector-databases-s062","q":"Which settings do you tune first when a vector search must be faster or more accurate?","a":"<p>The search-time parameters, since they need no rebuild: <code>nprobe</code> for IVF or <code>ef</code> for HNSW (higher means better recall and slower). Then index choice and build parameters (<code>nlist</code>, <code>M</code>, <code>efConstruction</code>), the number of candidates (<code>limit</code>), filters and partitions, and finally the embedding dimension. Measure recall against an exact FLAT baseline. Lower values trade recall for latency. Also request fewer <code>output_fields</code>, set <code>ignore_growing=True</code> if fresh data may be missed, and check that the collection is loaded and the index is built.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0272","card":"q-vector-databases-064-monitoring-and-maintenance","sec":"vector-databases-s063","q":"What should you monitor in a production vector search service?","a":"<p>Search latency (percentiles such as p95 and p99, not only the average), throughput and error rate, recall or relevance on a test set, memory and disk use, index build status, collection size, ingestion lag, and data quality (duplicates, stale entries). Milvus exposes metrics for Prometheus and Grafana.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0273","card":"q-vector-databases-064-monitoring-and-maintenance","sec":"vector-databases-s063","q":"Why is average latency a poor monitoring number for a search service?","a":"<p>Averages hide the slow tail. A few slow requests (cold cache, big filter, compaction) barely move the mean but users notice them. Track percentiles (p95, p99) and a histogram, and separate the embedding time from the vector search time.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>avg_latency = total_time / iterations</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0274","card":"q-vector-databases-064-monitoring-and-maintenance","sec":"vector-databases-s063","q":"What are the problems with this data-quality check?","a":"<p>The query returns the first N rows it finds, not a random sample, which can be unrepresentative. The requirements for the filter expression depend on the Milvus version, so use an explicit expression such as a primary-key range. It also moves large vectors over the network. Sample by id ranges, and compare pairs with a similarity search, not an O(n^2) loop.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>results = collection.query(expr=&quot;&quot;, limit=sample_size, output_fields=[&quot;embedding&quot;])</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0275","card":"q-vector-databases-064-monitoring-and-maintenance","sec":"vector-databases-s063","q":"What routine maintenance does a vector database collection need?","a":"<p>Remove duplicates and stale entries, run compaction to reclaim space after deletes, rebuild or tune the index when the data grows or changes a lot, re-embed when the embedding model changes (new collection, then switch), take backups, and re-evaluate quality on a test set after changes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0276","card":"q-vector-databases-065-alternative-vector-dbs","sec":"vector-databases-s064","q":"How do FAISS and a vector database such as Milvus or Qdrant differ?","a":"<p>FAISS is a library for similarity search in memory, with many index algorithms and GPU support. It has no server, persistence, access control, replication, or built-in metadata filtering and updates. A vector database wraps ANN search with storage, CRUD, filtering, scaling and operations. Use FAISS for embedded or research use, a database for production services.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0277","card":"q-vector-databases-065-alternative-vector-dbs","sec":"vector-databases-s064","q":"What are the main differences between Pinecone, Weaviate, Qdrant and Chroma, and how do you choose?","a":"<p>Pinecone is a fully managed closed-source service (fast to start, no ops, vendor lock-in). Weaviate is open source with built-in hybrid (keyword plus vector) search and modules. Qdrant (written in Rust) is open source with strong payload filtering. Chroma is a lightweight, developer-friendly store for prototypes and local work. Choose by scale, filtering needs, ops capacity, hosting rules and cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0278","card":"q-vector-databases-065-alternative-vector-dbs","sec":"vector-databases-s064","q":"In recent Pinecone Python clients (v3 and later), how do you create a client, unlike the older <code>pinecone.init(...)</code> style?","a":"<p><code>pinecone.init</code> belongs to the older client. From v3, you create a client object, <code>from pinecone import Pinecone; pc = Pinecone(api_key=...)</code>, and get an index with <code>pc.Index(\"your-index\")</code>. Always check the client version, because vector database clients change their APIs often.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import pinecone\npinecone.init(api_key=&quot;your-key&quot;, environment=&quot;us-west1-gcp&quot;)\nindex = pinecone.Index(&quot;your-index&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0279","card":"q-vector-databases-065-alternative-vector-dbs","sec":"vector-databases-s064","q":"What is the difference between <code>chromadb.Client()</code> and a persistent Chroma client?","a":"<p><code>chromadb.Client()</code> is an ephemeral in-memory client, so data is lost when the process ends. <code>chromadb.PersistentClient(path=...)</code> stores data on disk. For production or multi-user use, run Chroma as a server or use a database built for it.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import chromadb\nclient = chromadb.Client()\ncollection = client.create_collection(&quot;docs&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0280","card":"q-vector-databases-065-alternative-vector-dbs","sec":"vector-databases-s064","q":"In the Weaviate Python client, what changed between v3 (<code>weaviate.Client</code>) and v4?","a":"<p><code>weaviate.Client(url)</code> is the v3 client. The v4 client (late 2023) uses helper connectors such as <code>weaviate.connect_to_local()</code> and a different collections API, and it should be closed when done. Code and tutorials written for v3 do not run unchanged on v4.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import weaviate\nclient = weaviate.Client(&quot;http://localhost:8080&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0281","card":"q-vector-databases-066-langchain-vector-store-abstraction-and-r","sec":"vector-databases-s065","q":"Why use LangChain's vector store interface instead of calling each vector database client directly?","a":"<p>One interface (<code>similarity_search</code>, <code>add_documents</code>, <code>as_retriever</code>) works over many stores, so you can swap Milvus, Chroma or Pinecone with little code change and plug the store into chains and agents. The costs are an extra layer, fewer store-specific features, and API churn between LangChain versions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0282","card":"q-vector-databases-066-langchain-vector-store-abstraction-and-r","sec":"vector-databases-s065","q":"What is the difference between <code>similarity_search</code> and <code>as_retriever</code> on a LangChain vector store?","a":"<p><code>similarity_search</code> is a direct call that returns documents. <code>as_retriever</code> wraps the store as a retriever object, the standard interface that chains and agents use, with settings such as <code>k</code> in <code>search_kwargs</code>. <code>similarity_search_with_score</code> also returns the distance.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>retriever = vector_stores[&quot;milvus&quot;].as_retriever(search_kwargs={&quot;k&quot;: 3})\ndocs = vector_stores[&quot;milvus&quot;].similarity_search(&quot;question&quot;, k=3)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0283","card":"q-vector-databases-066-langchain-vector-store-abstraction-and-r","sec":"vector-databases-s065","q":"In LangChain's <code>RetrievalQA.from_chain_type</code>, what does <code>chain_type=\"stuff\"</code> mean, and what are the alternatives?","a":"<p><code>stuff</code> puts all retrieved documents into one prompt: simple, one LLM call, but limited by the context window. <code>map_reduce</code> summarises or answers each document separately and then combines the results; <code>refine</code> goes through the documents iteratively, improving the answer; <code>map_rerank</code> scores answers per document and keeps the best. The last three use more LLM calls.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>qa_chain = RetrievalQA.from_chain_type(llm=llm, chain_type=&quot;stuff&quot;, retriever=retriever)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0284","card":"q-vector-databases-066-langchain-vector-store-abstraction-and-r","sec":"vector-databases-s065","q":"In LangChain 0.2 and later, which API replaces <code>RetrievalQA</code> and <code>chain.run</code>?","a":"<p><code>RetrievalQA</code> is marked deprecated in favour of <code>create_retrieval_chain</code> (with <code>create_stuff_documents_chain</code>) or LCEL composition, and <code>chain.run(...)</code> was replaced by <code>.invoke(...)</code>. Vector stores and LLM wrappers moved from <code>langchain</code> to <code>langchain_community</code> and partner packages. Pin the LangChain version used by examples.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0285","card":"q-vector-databases-067-llamaindex","sec":"vector-databases-s066","q":"What does each piece do in this LlamaIndex pipeline?","a":"<p><code>SimpleDirectoryReader</code> loads files into <code>Document</code> objects. <code>MilvusVectorStore</code> is the storage backend. <code>VectorStoreIndex.from_documents</code> splits the documents into nodes, embeds them and writes them to the store. <code>as_query_engine(similarity_top_k=3)</code> builds a RAG query engine that retrieves 3 nodes, builds the prompt and calls the LLM, and <code>query</code> returns the answer.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>documents = SimpleDirectoryReader(&quot;./data&quot;).load_data()\nvector_store = MilvusVectorStore(host=&quot;localhost&quot;, port=19530, collection_name=&quot;documents&quot;)\nindex = VectorStoreIndex.from_documents(documents, vector_store=vector_store)\nquery_engine = index.as_query_engine(similarity_top_k=3)\nresponse = query_engine.query(&quot;Explain the key concepts&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0286","card":"q-vector-databases-067-llamaindex","sec":"vector-databases-s066","q":"How do LlamaIndex and LangChain differ, and how do you choose?","a":"<p>LlamaIndex centres on data: ingestion, indexing, retrieval and query engines for RAG over your documents, with many connectors. LangChain is a broader framework for chains, agents, tools and integrations. Both can do RAG and can be used together. Pick LlamaIndex for a data-heavy RAG focus, LangChain (or LangGraph) for complex workflows and agents.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0287","card":"q-vector-databases-067-llamaindex","sec":"vector-databases-s066","q":"In LlamaIndex 0.10 and later, how do 0.9-style imports such as <code>from llama_index import VectorStoreIndex</code> and <code>llama_index.vector_stores</code> change?","a":"<p>Since v0.10 the core moved to <code>llama_index.core</code> (<code>from llama_index.core import VectorStoreIndex, SimpleDirectoryReader</code>), and integrations are separate packages, for example <code>llama_index.vector_stores.milvus</code> from <code>llama-index-vector-stores-milvus</code>. Old import paths from 0.9 and earlier fail on new versions.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from llama_index import VectorStoreIndex, SimpleDirectoryReader\nfrom llama_index.vector_stores import MilvusVectorStore</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0288","card":"q-vector-databases-067-llamaindex","sec":"vector-databases-s066","q":"What is a node in LlamaIndex, and why are documents split into nodes?","a":"<p>A node is a chunk of a document with text, metadata and relationships to neighbouring nodes. Documents are split into nodes so each can be embedded and retrieved on its own, which is the same reason RAG uses chunks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0289","card":"q-vector-databases-068-enterprise-rag-application-config-driven","sec":"vector-databases-s067","q":"Why put RAG settings such as the embedding model, chunk size and threshold into a config object?","a":"<p>The pieces that must stay consistent (embedding model, chunk size, collection, threshold) live in one place, are versioned and can differ per environment (dev, staging, production) without code changes. It also makes experiments and A/B tests easy, and lets you record which settings built an index. Secrets stay out of it: read them from the environment.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>@dataclass\nclass RAGConfig:\n    vector_db_host: str = &quot;localhost&quot;\n    collection_name: str = &quot;enterprise_docs&quot;\n    embedding_model: str = &quot;text-embedding-ada-002&quot;\n    chunk_size: int = 512\n    similarity_threshold: float = 0.3</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0290","card":"q-vector-databases-068-enterprise-rag-application-config-driven","sec":"vector-databases-s067","q":"What is wrong with this glob pattern in a LangChain <code>DirectoryLoader</code>?","a":"<p>Python's <code>pathlib</code> globbing does not support brace expansion (<code>{pdf,txt,md}</code>), so it matches no files and the pipeline silently loads nothing. Use one pattern per extension (several loaders) or filter the file list yourself. It is worth asserting that the document count is greater than zero.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>loader = DirectoryLoader(path, glob=&quot;**/*.{pdf,txt,md}&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0291","card":"q-vector-databases-068-enterprise-rag-application-config-driven","sec":"vector-databases-s067","q":"What happens if the document pipeline calls <code>vector_store.add_documents(documents)</code> every time it runs?","a":"<p>Documents get added again every run, so the store fills with duplicates and retrieval returns repeated chunks. Provide stable ids so that the store updates existing entries, or track processed files and delete old chunks of changed documents before adding.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0292","card":"q-vector-databases-068-enterprise-rag-application-config-driven","sec":"vector-databases-s067","q":"Why does a <code>similarity_threshold</code> passed to a retrieval call mean different things in different vector stores?","a":"<p>Some stores return a distance (lower is better), others a similarity score (higher is better), and the scale depends on the metric. The same number <code>0.3</code> can be strict in one and useless in another, so check the semantics of the store and metric before reusing a threshold.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0293","card":"q-vector-databases-068-enterprise-rag-application-config-driven","sec":"vector-databases-s067","q":"Why create the vector store, LLM and splitter in a setup method fed from the config, instead of inside each query?","a":"<p>Clients and models are created once and reused, so each query does not pay connection and initialisation costs. It also makes components replaceable and testable, because tests can pass a different config or a fake store.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0294","card":"q-vector-databases-069-advanced-features","sec":"vector-databases-s068","q":"Why is it a poor idea to send the raw chat history together with the new question to the retriever in a conversational RAG system?","a":"<p>The embedding of a long mixed text is dominated by the history and diluted, so retrieval quality drops, and the prompt grows. The usual approach is to rewrite the follow-up into a standalone question with the LLM (\"and its price?\" becomes \"What is the price of product X?\"), retrieve with that, and give the history to the generation step only if needed.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>enhanced_query = f&quot;Previous conversation: {history}\\nCurrent question: {message}&quot;\nresponse = self.rag.query(enhanced_query)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"vector-databases-f0295","card":"q-vector-databases-069-advanced-features","sec":"vector-databases-s068","q":"In LangChain, what does <code>ConversationBufferWindowMemory(k=5)</code> do, and what is the trade-off?","a":"<p>It keeps only the last 5 exchanges of a conversation and drops older ones. This bounds the prompt size and cost, but the bot forgets earlier details. Alternatives are summarising older turns or storing facts in long-term memory. In LangChain 0.3 these legacy memory classes are deprecated in favour of message history on runnables and LangGraph persistence.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0296","card":"q-vector-databases-069-advanced-features","sec":"vector-databases-s068","q":"How would you build multi-modal RAG over documents containing text and images?","a":"<p>Option 1: use a multi-modal embedding model such as CLIP that places text and images in the same vector space, so a text query can retrieve images. Option 2: convert images to text (captions, OCR, a vision-LLM description) and index them as text. Keep metadata linking each image to its page. Pass retrieved images to a vision-capable LLM for the answer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0297","card":"q-vector-databases-069-advanced-features","sec":"vector-databases-s068","q":"Why do two separate embedding models and stores for text and images not give one ranked list in multi-modal RAG?","a":"<p>Vectors from different models live in different spaces, so their distances cannot be compared or merged. You need a shared embedding space (CLIP-style), or search each store and then merge with a reranker or a score normalisation you validate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"vector-databases-f0298","card":"q-vector-databases-069-advanced-features","sec":"vector-databases-s068","q":"What would you log and evaluate in a production RAG system?","a":"<p>Log per request: the query (mind privacy and redact personal data), retrieved chunk ids and scores, prompt size in tokens, the response, stage latencies, errors and user feedback. Then evaluate offline with a labelled set (retrieval metrics and answer faithfulness) and watch trends. Logging the response length alone is not enough to debug a bad answer.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>self.logger.info({&quot;query&quot;: query, &quot;response_length&quot;: len(response),\n                  &quot;latency&quot;: latency, &quot;timestamp&quot;: time.time()})</code></pre></div>"}];
